WEBVTT

1
00:00:47,005 --> 00:00:50,592
{\an8}There she is!
Bea Bishop, my girl!

2
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:52,552
{\an8}Ready to dive back into
the grit of live theatre?

3
00:00:52,594 --> 00:00:53,720
{\an8}Hi, Mom.

4
00:00:53,762 --> 00:00:56,222
{\an8}Everybody is so excited
to see you direct

5
00:00:56,264 --> 00:00:57,932
{\an8}<i>The Taming of the Shrew.</i>

6
00:00:57,974 --> 00:00:59,309
{\an8}Where is your TV star?

7
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:01,394
{\an8}Her name is Hannah,
not "TV Star."

8
00:01:01,436 --> 00:01:03,021
{\an8}I know that.

9
00:01:03,063 --> 00:01:05,774
{\an8}Award-winning actress
Hannah Michaels!

10
00:01:05,815 --> 00:01:07,734
{\an8}The coup of this summer's
Shakespeare Festival,

11
00:01:07,776 --> 00:01:09,444
{\an8}thanks to you!

12
00:01:09,486 --> 00:01:11,279
{\an8}Publicity's ramping up
promotion.

13
00:01:11,321 --> 00:01:13,740
{\an8}Well, when she said
that she wanted to do a play

14
00:01:13,782 --> 00:01:15,617
{\an8}during her break, I knew
it was a great opportunity

15
00:01:15,658 --> 00:01:16,701
{\an8}for both of us.

16
00:01:16,743 --> 00:01:18,203
{\an8}By the way, thank you so much

17
00:01:18,244 --> 00:01:19,871
{\an8}for scheduling our play
last in the season

18
00:01:19,913 --> 00:01:21,331
{\an8}so that she would have
an opportunity

19
00:01:21,372 --> 00:01:22,540
{\an8}to finish shooting her series.

20
00:01:22,582 --> 00:01:24,626
{\an8}Well, the main show
in the outdoor theatre

21
00:01:24,667 --> 00:01:27,003
{\an8}is always the last one up,
so it worked out.

22
00:01:27,045 --> 00:01:30,590
{\an8}You got four weeks rehearsals,
and then your play opens.

23
00:01:30,632 --> 00:01:32,717
{\an8}I'm ready.

24
00:01:32,759 --> 00:01:34,719
{\an8}- You're finally home.
- I'm thrilled.

25
00:01:34,761 --> 00:01:38,097
{\an8}I love that with live theatre
anything can happen.

26
00:01:38,139 --> 00:01:41,267
{\an8}Well, theatre is in your blood.

27
00:01:41,309 --> 00:01:44,646
{\an8}I'll never forget little you
running around backstage

28
00:01:44,687 --> 00:01:48,108
{\an8}asking all the stagehands
to let you open the curtain.

29
00:01:48,149 --> 00:01:50,944
{\an8}Oh, I was so precocious.

30
00:01:50,985 --> 00:01:53,071
{\an8}Everybody loved
having you around.

31
00:01:53,112 --> 00:01:56,074
{\an8}Especially after your dad left.

32
00:01:56,115 --> 00:01:58,368
{\an8}It was just you and me
against the world.

33
00:01:58,409 --> 00:02:00,745
{\an8}Yeah, what a time.

34
00:02:00,787 --> 00:02:02,038
And now you're back.

35
00:02:02,080 --> 00:02:04,165
And you have
charted your own path.

36
00:02:04,207 --> 00:02:06,000
Yeah, out of your shadow, Mom.

37
00:02:06,042 --> 00:02:08,253
I couldn't be prouder.

38
00:02:08,294 --> 00:02:10,964
So, Hannah is, uh,
meeting the rest of the cast

39
00:02:11,005 --> 00:02:13,341
at the tavern?

40
00:02:13,383 --> 00:02:14,759
Yes.

41
00:02:14,801 --> 00:02:17,095
Yeah, I thought it would be
a good, cozy place

42
00:02:17,137 --> 00:02:18,555
to introduce her
to her castmates.

43
00:02:18,596 --> 00:02:20,306
By the way, I didn't see
anybody listed

44
00:02:20,348 --> 00:02:22,517
- for the role of Petruchio.
- Right, I'm...

45
00:02:22,559 --> 00:02:25,019
It's a tough part to cast
because he's the male

46
00:02:25,061 --> 00:02:26,646
romantic lead of the show,

47
00:02:26,688 --> 00:02:29,816
so you need a strong actor
with presence, charisma.

48
00:02:29,858 --> 00:02:32,277
But he's also the kind of guy
that you fall in love with,

49
00:02:32,318 --> 00:02:33,570
even though he drives you nuts.

50
00:02:33,611 --> 00:02:34,863
When's the next round
of auditions?

51
00:02:34,904 --> 00:02:36,865
Actually,
I've already cast him, darling.

52
00:02:36,906 --> 00:02:38,783
Oh!
So, who's playing Petruchio?

53
00:02:38,825 --> 00:02:40,285
Hey, how are ya?

54
00:02:40,326 --> 00:02:42,579
Oh, Benny, my dear.

55
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:44,080
Back from the national tour.

56
00:02:44,122 --> 00:02:45,748
How did your <i>Death of a Salesman</i>

57
00:02:45,790 --> 00:02:48,585
play before audiences?

58
00:02:48,626 --> 00:02:50,378
Sellouts everywhere,
rave reviews.

59
00:02:50,420 --> 00:02:52,005
As I would have expected.

60
00:02:52,046 --> 00:02:53,840
But I'm almost relieved
to come home to Shakespeare

61
00:02:53,882 --> 00:02:54,883
every season.

62
00:02:54,924 --> 00:02:55,884
Hi, Benny.

63
00:02:55,925 --> 00:02:57,260
Hey.

64
00:02:58,344 --> 00:02:59,721
Bea?

65
00:02:59,762 --> 00:03:02,849
Um, you're...
visiting your mom.

66
00:03:02,891 --> 00:03:04,601
That's... that's nice.

67
00:03:04,642 --> 00:03:06,102
How long you in town for?

68
00:03:06,144 --> 00:03:08,521
Oh, no, I'm actually directing
<i>Taming of the Shrew.</i>

69
00:03:08,563 --> 00:03:09,689
- Oh.
- Yeah.

70
00:03:09,731 --> 00:03:11,482
Well, that's news to me.

71
00:03:11,524 --> 00:03:14,944
I'm playing Petruchio
in <i>Taming of the Shrew.</i>

72
00:03:14,986 --> 00:03:16,821
That is news to me.

73
00:03:16,863 --> 00:03:20,408
You both know I can't resist
a dramatic reveal.

74
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:21,951
- No, no...
- Yeah, no, it's fine.

75
00:03:21,993 --> 00:03:23,119
Couldn't care less.

76
00:03:23,161 --> 00:03:24,996
This season
has to end with a bang.

77
00:03:25,038 --> 00:03:27,457
The final show is the one
that everybody waits for.

78
00:03:27,498 --> 00:03:30,126
We've already got a buzz
going on about Hannah Michaels,

79
00:03:30,168 --> 00:03:32,378
and we have record sign-ups

80
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:34,756
for this year's Young Friends
of the Fest events.

81
00:03:35,965 --> 00:03:37,717
And Benny, would you reconsider

82
00:03:37,759 --> 00:03:39,552
teaching at
the actors' workshop?

83
00:03:39,594 --> 00:03:40,887
We've got a waiting list.

84
00:03:40,929 --> 00:03:43,139
You know, you're kind of
a legend around here.

85
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:44,265
No.

86
00:03:44,307 --> 00:03:46,184
Thank you.
Uh...

87
00:03:46,225 --> 00:03:48,061
I don't wanna
spread myself too thin.

88
00:03:48,102 --> 00:03:50,188
Right.
The play's the thing.

89
00:03:51,981 --> 00:03:54,567
And you two,
back together again.

90
00:03:54,609 --> 00:03:58,780
I have full faith that you're
going to be able to set your...

91
00:03:58,821 --> 00:04:00,740
differing views aside.

92
00:04:00,782 --> 00:04:03,034
It's been so long since
I was here directing a play.

93
00:04:03,076 --> 00:04:04,243
Five years.

94
00:04:04,285 --> 00:04:06,120
What's that, uh,
<i>Winter's Tale</i> quote,

95
00:04:06,162 --> 00:04:07,455
"What's past is prologue"?

96
00:04:07,497 --> 00:04:09,791
That's from <i>The Tempest.</i>

97
00:04:09,832 --> 00:04:12,126
- Close enough.
- Mm, not really.

98
00:04:12,168 --> 00:04:13,753
And it's settled, then.

99
00:04:13,795 --> 00:04:16,381
Bea, I really have to have
a private word with you.

100
00:04:16,422 --> 00:04:19,133
Mom, can it wait?
I have my...

101
00:04:19,175 --> 00:04:22,637
<i>We</i> have our first
read-through of the play.

102
00:04:22,679 --> 00:04:24,430
- Actually, it can't.
- Oh.

103
00:04:24,472 --> 00:04:26,224
Yeah, sure, I'll be...
I'll be right there.

104
00:04:30,645 --> 00:04:33,147
I'll see you at the tavern,
then, soon.

105
00:04:33,189 --> 00:04:34,565
Wouldn't miss it.

106
00:04:34,607 --> 00:04:36,943
I put in a special order
of Macbeth's Scotch Eggs.

107
00:04:36,985 --> 00:04:38,569
Get everyone
in the Shakespeare mood.

108
00:04:38,611 --> 00:04:40,279
I'm bringing pizza.

109
00:04:40,321 --> 00:04:41,281
Perfect.

110
00:05:08,141 --> 00:05:09,684
What's going on?
Are you okay?

111
00:05:09,726 --> 00:05:11,185
Oh, please.

112
00:05:11,227 --> 00:05:13,062
I power-walked a 5K this morning

113
00:05:13,104 --> 00:05:15,106
before you even
rolled out of bed.

114
00:05:15,148 --> 00:05:16,774
Of course you did.

115
00:05:16,816 --> 00:05:17,817
So, what's up?

116
00:05:19,152 --> 00:05:20,611
I am...

117
00:05:20,653 --> 00:05:22,613
retiring, Bea.

118
00:05:22,655 --> 00:05:25,033
At the end
of this festival season.

119
00:05:26,326 --> 00:05:27,702
Wow.

120
00:05:27,744 --> 00:05:28,911
Mom, are you sure?

121
00:05:28,953 --> 00:05:30,997
I mean, I knew
it was a possibility...

122
00:05:31,039 --> 00:05:33,958
You know I've given my
whole life to this festival.

123
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:36,002
My heart, my soul...

124
00:05:36,044 --> 00:05:37,295
It's your baby.

125
00:05:37,336 --> 00:05:39,338
No, you're my baby.

126
00:05:41,341 --> 00:05:44,844
- But the festival is...
- Your legacy.

127
00:05:44,886 --> 00:05:49,515
Yeah, but you can't really
have a legacy unless you leave.

128
00:05:49,557 --> 00:05:51,100
So, it's time.

129
00:05:52,769 --> 00:05:55,688
Well, Mom, I have
a crazy idea to pitch you.

130
00:05:55,730 --> 00:05:57,190
Oh, I bet.

131
00:05:57,231 --> 00:05:59,192
Don't try to stop me.
Let me just say it out loud.

132
00:05:59,233 --> 00:06:00,318
Okay, Bea.

133
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:01,402
I wanna take over the festival.

134
00:06:01,444 --> 00:06:02,612
Yes.
Bea...

135
00:06:02,653 --> 00:06:04,697
Before you argue,
let me tell you why.

136
00:06:04,739 --> 00:06:06,491
Okay, wow.
Okay.

137
00:06:06,532 --> 00:06:09,243
I have been working here
since I was born.

138
00:06:09,285 --> 00:06:11,037
I know this place
inside and out.

139
00:06:11,079 --> 00:06:12,288
I've learned
from the very best...

140
00:06:12,330 --> 00:06:13,956
from you.

141
00:06:13,998 --> 00:06:16,125
I have the grace and I have
the expertise that's necessary.

142
00:06:16,167 --> 00:06:17,668
- Yes, Bea.
- I've been finding

143
00:06:17,710 --> 00:06:19,253
new, exciting ways
to share Shakespeare

144
00:06:19,295 --> 00:06:20,880
with a younger audience.

145
00:06:20,922 --> 00:06:23,674
Yes, Bea, stop talking
long enough to hear me.

146
00:06:23,716 --> 00:06:26,302
When somebody says yes,
you can stop pitching.

147
00:06:27,804 --> 00:06:29,806
Wait, but, Mom, I thought
you would feel like it was...

148
00:06:29,847 --> 00:06:31,265
No, Bea, you're right.

149
00:06:31,307 --> 00:06:33,726
It is your destiny, sweetheart.

150
00:06:33,768 --> 00:06:36,479
I was just worried that
maybe you wouldn't want it

151
00:06:36,521 --> 00:06:38,898
because of your
TV directing career.

152
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:40,817
I want it.

153
00:06:40,858 --> 00:06:43,236
I want it badly.
Yes.

154
00:06:43,277 --> 00:06:45,738
- Yes?
- Yes, yes, yes!

155
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:47,532
I have a meeting with
the board president Juan

156
00:06:47,573 --> 00:06:49,617
next week.
You should be there.

157
00:06:49,659 --> 00:06:51,786
Anything you need.
Let's go meet with Juan.

158
00:06:51,828 --> 00:06:53,621
I'm so glad
to hear you say this.

159
00:06:53,663 --> 00:06:54,997
I'm so happy for you.

160
00:06:55,039 --> 00:06:56,707
Mom...

161
00:06:56,749 --> 00:06:58,918
And I'm ready.

162
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:01,087
I have to go and introduce
Hannah to the cast

163
00:07:01,129 --> 00:07:03,256
- at the tavern, so I'm gonna go.
- Off you go.

164
00:07:03,297 --> 00:07:05,007
- The play's the thing.
- Play's the thing.

165
00:07:09,303 --> 00:07:11,222
Hannah, hi!

166
00:07:11,264 --> 00:07:13,766
- Bea!
- Hi!

167
00:07:13,808 --> 00:07:16,978
Oh, thank you so much
for agreeing to do this.

168
00:07:17,019 --> 00:07:18,813
Are you kidding? I've always
wanted to do theatre,

169
00:07:18,855 --> 00:07:19,981
but I've never had the chance.

170
00:07:20,022 --> 00:07:21,941
You're gonna be so great.

171
00:07:21,983 --> 00:07:23,943
Well, this play is gonna
help people see me

172
00:07:23,985 --> 00:07:25,987
- as a more serious actress.
- That's what you are.

173
00:07:26,028 --> 00:07:28,072
So, how's
the memorization going?

174
00:07:28,114 --> 00:07:29,532
It's a lot of lines.

175
00:07:29,574 --> 00:07:31,117
Yeah, it's coming along.
Yeah, almost there.

176
00:07:31,159 --> 00:07:32,451
Okay.

177
00:07:32,493 --> 00:07:34,495
I cannot wait to meet
the rest of the cast.

178
00:07:34,537 --> 00:07:35,997
They're so great.
They can't wait either.

179
00:07:36,038 --> 00:07:38,332
- Ooh, fun! Can I have a beer?
- No, you cannot.

180
00:07:38,374 --> 00:07:39,459
This is a professional
environment.

181
00:07:39,500 --> 00:07:40,877
Well, you're the one who decided

182
00:07:40,918 --> 00:07:42,420
to have a rehearsal in a pub.

183
00:07:42,461 --> 00:07:43,754
Okay, the read-through
was supposed to begin

184
00:07:43,796 --> 00:07:45,006
10 minutes ago.

185
00:07:45,047 --> 00:07:46,924
Meet Doug Berrie.
This is our stage manager.

186
00:07:46,966 --> 00:07:48,593
Pleased to meet you,
Ms. Michaels.

187
00:07:48,634 --> 00:07:50,219
Oh, Doug, please call me Hannah.

188
00:07:50,261 --> 00:07:51,429
- Mm-hm.
- Please.

189
00:07:51,471 --> 00:07:53,097
Yeah, I will call you
whatever you want,

190
00:07:53,139 --> 00:07:55,349
as long as you sign in
on time every day.

191
00:07:55,391 --> 00:07:57,435
- He's funny.
- Yeah, I'm not joking.

192
00:07:57,476 --> 00:07:59,145
Time's ticking.
Here we go.

193
00:07:59,187 --> 00:08:00,480
Pitter-patter, come on.

194
00:08:00,521 --> 00:08:02,815
Okay.
He cares a lot.

195
00:08:02,857 --> 00:08:04,567
Now, listen,
you're the new guy here,

196
00:08:04,609 --> 00:08:07,278
so it's okay if it takes you
a while to warm up to them.

197
00:08:07,320 --> 00:08:08,696
Hi, everybody!

198
00:08:08,738 --> 00:08:10,781
- I'd say she's all warmed up.
- Yeah, that was easy.

199
00:08:10,823 --> 00:08:13,159
Um, Hannah, I'd like
to introduce you to Maggie.

200
00:08:13,201 --> 00:08:15,203
She's playing your sister,
Bianca.

201
00:08:15,244 --> 00:08:16,996
She's also your understudy
as Kate.

202
00:08:17,038 --> 00:08:19,540
- Hi, Baya!
- Oh, it's actually "Bee-a."

203
00:08:19,582 --> 00:08:20,833
Short for Beatrice.

204
00:08:20,875 --> 00:08:23,169
Like...
"To Be-a or not to Be-a."

205
00:08:23,211 --> 00:08:26,047
Right. So sorry.
It's only my second year.

206
00:08:26,088 --> 00:08:27,548
It's totally fine.
Happens all the time.

207
00:08:27,590 --> 00:08:29,842
This is Don.
He's playing one of the suitors.

208
00:08:29,884 --> 00:08:32,011
And this is Clay.

209
00:08:32,053 --> 00:08:34,388
He's gonna be playing
another one of your suitors.

210
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:36,474
Hi, there.
I'm Hannah.

211
00:08:36,515 --> 00:08:38,309
I know.
Nice to meet you.

212
00:08:40,645 --> 00:08:43,856
You know, Hortensio and Kate
don't have many scenes together.

213
00:08:43,898 --> 00:08:45,691
- That's a shame.
- Alright, everybody.

214
00:08:45,733 --> 00:08:47,818
I took the liberty
of ordering us

215
00:08:47,860 --> 00:08:50,404
a couple of rounds
of Macbeth's Scotch Eggs.

216
00:08:50,446 --> 00:08:52,865
Get us in the mood.
Joy?

217
00:08:52,907 --> 00:08:55,409
I'd like to say a few words,

218
00:08:55,451 --> 00:08:58,371
but we are actually waiting
for our leading man, Petruchio...

219
00:08:58,412 --> 00:09:00,623
Hello!
Team Shakespeare Fest!

220
00:09:00,665 --> 00:09:02,833
I've returned
from the touring war

221
00:09:02,875 --> 00:09:04,627
that is the <i>Death of a Salesman,</i>

222
00:09:04,669 --> 00:09:07,713
and I brought riches
from our plunder!

223
00:09:07,755 --> 00:09:10,049
It's pizza, Benny.
Just say you brought pizza.

224
00:09:10,091 --> 00:09:12,593
I brought pizza!

225
00:09:12,635 --> 00:09:14,512
Last tour stop was New Haven,

226
00:09:14,553 --> 00:09:17,723
and I got classic
Sal's Pizza for everyone!

227
00:09:17,765 --> 00:09:19,308
Mmm, still warm!

228
00:09:19,350 --> 00:09:21,060
We were just talking about this!

229
00:09:21,102 --> 00:09:22,353
Oh-ho-ho-ho-ho.

230
00:09:24,647 --> 00:09:26,649
Benny, I was just about
to say a few words.

231
00:09:26,691 --> 00:09:28,067
You can't say words

232
00:09:28,109 --> 00:09:29,944
and enjoy a perfect slice
at the same time?

233
00:09:29,986 --> 00:09:31,779
Well, I wouldn't wanna
talk with my mouth full.

234
00:09:31,821 --> 00:09:32,989
Well, you're missing out.

235
00:09:33,030 --> 00:09:34,699
You haven't changed a bit,
Gersten.

236
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:36,284
Why mess with perfection?

237
00:09:36,325 --> 00:09:38,661
Whoever called you perfect
was lying to your pretty face.

238
00:09:38,703 --> 00:09:41,080
Oh?
You think I have a pretty face?

239
00:09:41,122 --> 00:09:43,165
Ah, except you got a little
pizza sauce right there.

240
00:09:43,207 --> 00:09:45,543
We can hear you guys,
just so you know.

241
00:09:45,585 --> 00:09:47,712
I think I actually
missed the low hum

242
00:09:47,753 --> 00:09:49,964
of you two squabbling.
Just like old times.

243
00:09:51,924 --> 00:09:53,301
Hannah,
I'd like to introduce you

244
00:09:53,342 --> 00:09:54,719
to Benny Gersten.

245
00:09:54,760 --> 00:09:57,805
He's playing your Petruchio.
I apologize in advance.

246
00:09:59,473 --> 00:10:00,975
- Right. Hannah.
- Yeah.

247
00:10:01,017 --> 00:10:02,226
- Nice to meet you.
- You too.

248
00:10:02,268 --> 00:10:03,394
Excuse me.

249
00:10:03,436 --> 00:10:04,979
I'm sorry...
Can we have a quick sidebar?

250
00:10:07,064 --> 00:10:11,235
Okay, uh, finish up your pizza
and then maybe let's team up

251
00:10:11,277 --> 00:10:14,572
for a warm-up.
Maybe Roses, Buds, Thorns?

252
00:10:14,614 --> 00:10:15,781
Wait, wait, roses and what?

253
00:10:15,823 --> 00:10:18,743
I'll be back in a sec.

254
00:10:18,784 --> 00:10:20,703
Okay, what is the deal
with those two?

255
00:10:20,745 --> 00:10:24,081
I heard just after college,
they played Romeo and Juliet

256
00:10:24,123 --> 00:10:26,250
and fell in love,
and then when the play ended,

257
00:10:26,292 --> 00:10:27,710
so did their relationship.

258
00:10:27,752 --> 00:10:29,128
Benny's a nomad actor,

259
00:10:29,170 --> 00:10:30,546
but his love of
classical Shakespeare

260
00:10:30,588 --> 00:10:32,048
always brings him back here.

261
00:10:32,089 --> 00:10:33,674
Whenever Bea
directs at the festival,

262
00:10:33,716 --> 00:10:35,259
this is the way it is with them.

263
00:10:35,301 --> 00:10:36,552
Interesting.

264
00:10:36,594 --> 00:10:38,888
I'm a little surprised that you

265
00:10:38,929 --> 00:10:40,639
wanted to direct
<i>Taming of the Shrew.</i>

266
00:10:40,681 --> 00:10:43,851
Given that it's about a daughter
being married off like property.

267
00:10:43,893 --> 00:10:46,020
I don't treat Shakespeare
as sacrosanct like you do.

268
00:10:46,062 --> 00:10:47,897
I'm gonna find a way
to make this play connect

269
00:10:47,938 --> 00:10:48,981
with today's audience.

270
00:10:49,023 --> 00:10:50,858
Historical context matters.

271
00:10:50,900 --> 00:10:53,611
This festival is a love letter
to classic Shakespeare.

272
00:10:53,653 --> 00:10:55,488
We respect it
by doing it classically.

273
00:10:55,529 --> 00:10:57,615
Well, all the parts
were played by men back then.

274
00:10:57,657 --> 00:10:59,617
Can't wait to see you
lace up in a corset

275
00:10:59,658 --> 00:11:01,285
to play Lady Macbeth
the classical way.

276
00:11:01,327 --> 00:11:03,746
Look, all I'm saying is nobody
wants to see modern dance

277
00:11:03,788 --> 00:11:05,539
with some Elizabethan words
thrown in.

278
00:11:05,581 --> 00:11:09,085
Or the story of Romeo,
the emo influencer,

279
00:11:09,126 --> 00:11:10,878
and Juliet,
a true crime podcaster.

280
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:12,922
See, I actually think
that sounds amazing.

281
00:11:12,963 --> 00:11:15,174
Of course it does.
To you.

282
00:11:15,216 --> 00:11:19,053
You set <i>A Midsummer Night's</i>
<i>Dream</i> at a college rave.

283
00:11:19,095 --> 00:11:20,930
You made
the entire audience dance,

284
00:11:20,971 --> 00:11:22,556
and I don't dance.

285
00:11:22,598 --> 00:11:24,016
Not even with a glow stick.

286
00:11:24,058 --> 00:11:26,102
You saw that production?

287
00:11:26,143 --> 00:11:28,562
And I... I have notes.

288
00:11:28,604 --> 00:11:31,190
Which is exactly why I shouldn't
be playing Petruchio.

289
00:11:31,232 --> 00:11:33,526
- Are you quitting on me?
- No, I'm not quitting.

290
00:11:33,567 --> 00:11:35,861
I'm just not playing the lead
in a show that you're directing.

291
00:11:35,903 --> 00:11:38,072
I'll, uh,
play some smaller role.

292
00:11:38,114 --> 00:11:41,534
It'll have to be a role swap.
Any suggestions?

293
00:11:41,575 --> 00:11:43,035
Clay.

294
00:11:43,077 --> 00:11:46,038
I think he's paid his dues
playing supporting roles.

295
00:11:46,080 --> 00:11:47,998
He's your best choice
to play opposite Hannah.

296
00:11:48,040 --> 00:11:49,417
You seem awfully sure of this.

297
00:11:49,458 --> 00:11:50,835
I know the company
better than you.

298
00:11:50,876 --> 00:11:52,002
Hey!

299
00:11:52,044 --> 00:11:53,838
It's not a criticism.
It's just a fact.

300
00:11:53,879 --> 00:11:55,881
You've been away
a long time, Bea.

301
00:12:00,886 --> 00:12:03,264
Quick update.
The role of Petruchio

302
00:12:03,305 --> 00:12:05,599
will now be played by Clay.

303
00:12:05,641 --> 00:12:07,059
Me...?

304
00:12:07,101 --> 00:12:08,561
You'll be great, Clay.

305
00:12:08,602 --> 00:12:10,980
You will.
I love it.

306
00:12:11,021 --> 00:12:12,898
And that means, Benny,

307
00:12:12,940 --> 00:12:16,152
I hereby grant you
the role of Hortensio.

308
00:12:16,193 --> 00:12:18,821
Oh!
A role I could do blindfolded

309
00:12:18,863 --> 00:12:20,656
with one hand
tied behind my back.

310
00:12:20,698 --> 00:12:22,741
You know, I've always thought
Hortensio was actually a dancer.

311
00:12:22,783 --> 00:12:23,993
Don't you dare.

312
00:12:24,034 --> 00:12:26,287
Okay, everyone,
grab your scripts and a water.

313
00:12:26,328 --> 00:12:28,748
We're gonna start
the table read.

314
00:12:28,789 --> 00:12:31,041
No, Hannah and Clay
as the two leads is much better.

315
00:12:31,083 --> 00:12:32,668
Thank you.

316
00:12:32,710 --> 00:12:33,961
Did you just thank me?

317
00:12:37,465 --> 00:12:39,717
What planet are you from,

318
00:12:39,759 --> 00:12:41,510
and what have you done
with Bea Bishop?

319
00:12:41,552 --> 00:12:42,803
Alright, tell me the truth.

320
00:12:42,845 --> 00:12:44,764
Why did you really
not wanna play Petruchio?

321
00:12:46,182 --> 00:12:47,892
I already told you.

322
00:12:47,933 --> 00:12:49,894
- You wanna know what I think?
- Not really.

323
00:12:49,935 --> 00:12:52,062
But I suspect
you're gonna tell me anyway.

324
00:12:52,104 --> 00:12:53,606
I think you were afraid
Hannah Michaels

325
00:12:53,647 --> 00:12:54,899
was gonna outshine you.

326
00:12:54,940 --> 00:12:56,609
Not at all.
It doesn't hurt me

327
00:12:56,650 --> 00:12:58,277
to see another actor
take the spotlight.

328
00:12:58,319 --> 00:13:00,070
Alright, well, then maybe
you just couldn't handle

329
00:13:00,112 --> 00:13:02,406
the idea of me directing you
in a large role.

330
00:13:02,448 --> 00:13:04,200
I guess now we'll never know.

331
00:13:04,241 --> 00:13:06,368
Everyone,
this first week of rehearsal

332
00:13:06,410 --> 00:13:08,913
might be our hardest yet.

333
00:13:08,954 --> 00:13:11,749
But I have faith
that if we work together,

334
00:13:11,791 --> 00:13:13,667
we're gonna make
something really special.

335
00:13:13,709 --> 00:13:14,710
Act One.

336
00:13:18,756 --> 00:13:20,174
Toni, you're looking well!

337
00:13:20,216 --> 00:13:21,717
Oh! Thank you.

338
00:13:21,759 --> 00:13:22,968
And you must be Baya!

339
00:13:23,010 --> 00:13:25,346
Uh, it's Bea, for Beatrice.

340
00:13:25,387 --> 00:13:26,764
Apologies, of course.

341
00:13:26,806 --> 00:13:29,058
My kid's a huge fan of
the police show you direct on.

342
00:13:29,099 --> 00:13:31,644
- Oh.
- "The... Newbie"?

343
00:13:31,685 --> 00:13:34,146
- Thank you, that's sweet.
- I myself don't watch TV.

344
00:13:34,188 --> 00:13:35,731
Hm...

345
00:13:35,773 --> 00:13:40,528
Uh, Juan,
before we get to our work today,

346
00:13:40,569 --> 00:13:44,031
um... I have some personal news.

347
00:13:45,533 --> 00:13:48,369
Watching this festival blossom
over the last 40 years

348
00:13:48,410 --> 00:13:52,164
into an institution
has been the honour of my life.

349
00:13:53,415 --> 00:13:56,919
But this season will be my last.

350
00:13:56,961 --> 00:13:59,088
My dear Toni,

351
00:13:59,129 --> 00:14:01,048
I speak on behalf of the board
when I say

352
00:14:01,090 --> 00:14:02,216
it's been our absolute pleasure.

353
00:14:02,258 --> 00:14:04,301
Your timing is impeccable.

354
00:14:04,343 --> 00:14:06,220
The board was gonna wait
until the season was over

355
00:14:06,262 --> 00:14:07,346
to present it to you,

356
00:14:07,388 --> 00:14:09,139
but if you're planning
to retire anyway,

357
00:14:09,181 --> 00:14:11,350
we received a buyout offer
for the festival.

358
00:14:11,392 --> 00:14:13,561
What?
From... from who?

359
00:14:13,602 --> 00:14:15,104
They prefer to remain anonymous,

360
00:14:15,145 --> 00:14:17,398
but I can say
it's a media company

361
00:14:17,439 --> 00:14:19,024
and a generous offer.

362
00:14:19,066 --> 00:14:20,776
Is this the same company
that bought out

363
00:14:20,818 --> 00:14:22,653
the Woodridge Festival
last spring?

364
00:14:22,695 --> 00:14:24,572
I can neither confirm nor deny.

365
00:14:24,613 --> 00:14:28,409
Do you know that they stream
every opening night online?

366
00:14:28,450 --> 00:14:29,994
God, the audacity.

367
00:14:30,035 --> 00:14:33,622
Downsized cast and crew,
the cut out shows.

368
00:14:33,664 --> 00:14:36,917
We ended up hiring several
of the people they laid off.

369
00:14:36,959 --> 00:14:39,879
Yeah, this festival,
it must stay independent.

370
00:14:39,920 --> 00:14:42,590
I want that too,
but I am only one vote.

371
00:14:42,631 --> 00:14:45,009
From the board's perspective,
if you leave

372
00:14:45,050 --> 00:14:46,885
and we don't have a proper
successor for festival head...

373
00:14:46,927 --> 00:14:48,971
We have a proper successor.

374
00:14:51,640 --> 00:14:53,267
My Bea.

375
00:14:56,353 --> 00:14:57,980
I wasn't aware
that was the plan.

376
00:14:58,022 --> 00:15:00,399
This is the new plan.

377
00:15:00,441 --> 00:15:03,861
I've been preparing for this
my entire life, Juan.

378
00:15:03,903 --> 00:15:05,029
Literally.

379
00:15:05,070 --> 00:15:07,489
Interesting.

380
00:15:07,531 --> 00:15:09,491
As you know, even though
you founded the festival, Toni,

381
00:15:09,533 --> 00:15:12,828
the board must unanimously
approve a new festival head.

382
00:15:12,870 --> 00:15:14,288
You need to
prove yourself capable

383
00:15:14,330 --> 00:15:15,706
these next few weeks, Bea.

384
00:15:15,748 --> 00:15:18,000
Of course.
Yes.

385
00:15:18,042 --> 00:15:21,962
And how, in your mind,
would I do that?

386
00:15:22,004 --> 00:15:24,131
The board will want to see how
<i>Taming of the Shrew</i>

387
00:15:24,173 --> 00:15:25,758
fares under your guidance.

388
00:15:25,799 --> 00:15:28,010
Like with your celebrity friend,
for instance.

389
00:15:28,052 --> 00:15:30,804
Well, Hannah Michaels is
an incredibly dedicated actress.

390
00:15:30,846 --> 00:15:33,599
She's diving into this character
with her whole heart.

391
00:15:33,641 --> 00:15:37,102
I... I can assure you, she's
way more than just a celebrity.

392
00:15:37,144 --> 00:15:39,438
Well,
that will be important to see.

393
00:15:39,480 --> 00:15:43,400
If it's a critical
and financial success,

394
00:15:43,442 --> 00:15:45,903
then there's no reason
not to approve you to take over.

395
00:15:47,363 --> 00:15:48,906
What in the world was that?

396
00:15:48,947 --> 00:15:50,157
It's bigger than me.

397
00:15:50,199 --> 00:15:51,283
It's bigger than us.

398
00:15:51,325 --> 00:15:53,077
Mom, this is bad.

399
00:15:53,118 --> 00:15:55,496
I called my agent this week
and I passed on directing

400
00:15:55,537 --> 00:15:57,373
more episodes of "The Newbie"
because I didn't know

401
00:15:57,414 --> 00:15:58,832
there were more hoops
to jump through.

402
00:15:58,874 --> 00:16:00,250
Well, there won't be

403
00:16:00,292 --> 00:16:02,294
once your <i>Taming of the Shrew</i>
is a smash hit.

404
00:16:02,336 --> 00:16:04,171
Which I can't guarantee!

405
00:16:05,422 --> 00:16:07,424
Oh, you've got this.

406
00:16:07,466 --> 00:16:08,926
And you heard Juan.

407
00:16:08,967 --> 00:16:11,428
I mean, if we don't nail
this final show of the season,

408
00:16:11,470 --> 00:16:13,806
they're gonna sell the festival.

409
00:16:13,847 --> 00:16:15,641
It's all got so much
pressure on it.

410
00:16:16,975 --> 00:16:18,936
We don't have another choice.

411
00:16:33,826 --> 00:16:37,996
Okay, welcome to our
second week of rehearsals.

412
00:16:38,038 --> 00:16:42,209
I have decided to set this play
on a stock trading room floor.

413
00:16:42,251 --> 00:16:44,503
I'm also exploring the idea
of blending modern

414
00:16:44,545 --> 00:16:46,547
and Elizabethan costumes.

415
00:16:46,588 --> 00:16:49,299
Stay with me; it's critical
that we keep these characters

416
00:16:49,341 --> 00:16:50,968
relevant to a modern audience.

417
00:16:51,009 --> 00:16:53,011
Good luck.

418
00:16:53,053 --> 00:16:54,638
- Benny...?
- Mm-hm?

419
00:16:54,680 --> 00:16:56,306
Something to share?

420
00:16:56,348 --> 00:16:59,351
Ah, in my experience,
Shakespeare done classically

421
00:16:59,393 --> 00:17:00,477
never steers you wrong.

422
00:17:02,604 --> 00:17:05,399
Just gimme one...
quick second.

423
00:17:05,441 --> 00:17:06,942
Can I talk to you, please?

424
00:17:06,984 --> 00:17:09,028
Just because you can't imagine
relevant ways

425
00:17:09,069 --> 00:17:10,571
of bringing Shakespeare
to the 21st century

426
00:17:10,612 --> 00:17:12,865
doesn't mean I can't.

427
00:17:12,906 --> 00:17:14,241
I respect tradition.

428
00:17:14,283 --> 00:17:16,702
I was taught tradition too,
just not traditionally.

429
00:17:16,744 --> 00:17:18,662
Learn the rules so you know
when to break them.

430
00:17:18,704 --> 00:17:20,247
That's the entire message
of the play.

431
00:17:20,289 --> 00:17:22,291
- No, it's not.
- Well, it is to me.

432
00:17:22,332 --> 00:17:24,376
Am I the only one seeing
electricity between those two?

433
00:17:24,418 --> 00:17:27,087
Oh, no.
We all see it.

434
00:17:27,129 --> 00:17:30,090
You know what? They'd make
a great couple, if only...

435
00:17:30,132 --> 00:17:32,634
If only they could agree on...

436
00:17:32,676 --> 00:17:33,802
anything?

437
00:17:33,844 --> 00:17:35,054
If Shakespeare were alive today,

438
00:17:35,095 --> 00:17:36,096
he would almost certainly...

439
00:17:36,138 --> 00:17:38,557
Love comes in many forms.

440
00:17:38,599 --> 00:17:40,184
Grab a snack while
they go another round?

441
00:17:40,225 --> 00:17:41,935
Yeah.

442
00:17:41,977 --> 00:17:43,395
Okay.

443
00:17:43,437 --> 00:17:46,273
- You want a revolution.
- You want dusty museum pieces!

444
00:17:46,315 --> 00:17:48,817
"Those who forget the past
are doomed to repeat it."

445
00:17:48,859 --> 00:17:51,820
Can we just start
the rehearsal, please?

446
00:17:51,862 --> 00:17:53,030
That would be difficult.

447
00:17:55,699 --> 00:17:57,326
Where did everyone go?

448
00:17:57,367 --> 00:17:59,995
Oh, probably to the tavern
for a pint and a round of darts.

449
00:18:00,037 --> 00:18:02,456
Maybe two, depending on how long
you two keep this nonsense up.

450
00:18:02,498 --> 00:18:03,665
Time's ticking.

451
00:18:03,707 --> 00:18:05,417
Can you please
get everybody back, Doug?

452
00:18:05,459 --> 00:18:07,211
Thank you, Doug.
I appreciate you, Doug.

453
00:18:07,252 --> 00:18:08,337
Okay.

454
00:18:10,214 --> 00:18:12,174
Come on,
what's a little creative debate

455
00:18:12,216 --> 00:18:14,301
between like-minded
artists, hmm?

456
00:18:14,343 --> 00:18:16,970
No, no.

457
00:18:17,012 --> 00:18:20,432
I don't have time for your...
your Gersten shenanigans.

458
00:18:20,474 --> 00:18:22,518
There's things going on
that you don't know about.

459
00:18:22,559 --> 00:18:24,812
I knew you were
keeping a secret.

460
00:18:26,438 --> 00:18:27,606
Come on.

461
00:18:29,525 --> 00:18:31,902
My mom's retiring...

462
00:18:31,944 --> 00:18:33,779
and I wanna take over
as festival head.

463
00:18:35,114 --> 00:18:36,323
Wow.

464
00:18:36,365 --> 00:18:39,326
But the board
has a buyout offer on the table.

465
00:18:39,368 --> 00:18:41,203
- They might sell the festival?
- They might.

466
00:18:41,245 --> 00:18:43,247
If they don't approve me
to take over,

467
00:18:43,288 --> 00:18:46,375
then the festival
as we know it is over.

468
00:18:46,416 --> 00:18:48,919
This play has to be a success,
Benny.

469
00:18:48,961 --> 00:18:52,131
I need your help,
for the festival's sake.

470
00:18:52,172 --> 00:18:53,549
We can't let it happen.

471
00:18:53,590 --> 00:18:56,885
- So, you'll help me?
- Of course I will.

472
00:18:56,927 --> 00:18:58,846
Truce?

473
00:18:58,887 --> 00:19:00,889
No, "truce" means
we'll fight again another day.

474
00:19:00,931 --> 00:19:03,600
Alright,
so, what's the right word?

475
00:19:03,642 --> 00:19:05,686
- Peace?
- Peace.

476
00:19:05,727 --> 00:19:08,272
Okay, well,
now we just need to figure out

477
00:19:08,313 --> 00:19:10,440
who's right about
how to make the show a success,

478
00:19:10,482 --> 00:19:11,483
you or me.

479
00:19:11,525 --> 00:19:12,901
Me.

480
00:19:12,943 --> 00:19:14,403
Ah.
What could go wrong?

481
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:22,536
So, how was your
first week and a half?

482
00:19:22,578 --> 00:19:25,163
Lovely.

483
00:19:25,205 --> 00:19:27,791
You getting any sleep at all?

484
00:19:27,833 --> 00:19:29,209
A little.

485
00:19:29,251 --> 00:19:31,962
I was up way too late
texting last night.

486
00:19:32,004 --> 00:19:33,630
Well, coffee, stat.

487
00:19:33,672 --> 00:19:35,465
Got a long day
of rehearsal ahead of us.

488
00:19:35,507 --> 00:19:37,634
By the way,
I hate to do this to you,

489
00:19:37,676 --> 00:19:39,595
but you're gonna have to have
all of your lines

490
00:19:39,636 --> 00:19:40,971
fully memorized by this weekend.

491
00:19:41,013 --> 00:19:42,181
Oh, yeah,
I'm already halfway there.

492
00:19:42,222 --> 00:19:44,224
That is, up to Act 2-ish.

493
00:19:44,266 --> 00:19:45,267
Hmm...

494
00:19:48,687 --> 00:19:50,063
Hannah's hilarious.

495
00:19:50,105 --> 00:19:53,275
She's taking witty text banter
to a whole new level.

496
00:19:53,317 --> 00:19:55,152
You two seem to have
really hit it off.

497
00:19:55,193 --> 00:19:58,155
Yeah, she's just funny
and down-to-earth, you know?

498
00:19:59,323 --> 00:20:01,241
I watched her show.

499
00:20:01,283 --> 00:20:03,285
She's a very talented actress.

500
00:20:03,327 --> 00:20:04,369
She's the whole package.

501
00:20:04,411 --> 00:20:05,913
Whoa...

502
00:20:05,954 --> 00:20:07,706
Easy there, Clay.

503
00:20:07,748 --> 00:20:09,958
Save your feelings
for the stage, buddy.

504
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,419
She's just...
super cool, is all.

505
00:20:12,461 --> 00:20:15,589
Sure, she's cool today,
tomorrow it's a crush,

506
00:20:15,631 --> 00:20:17,215
in a week
you're head over heels,

507
00:20:17,257 --> 00:20:20,010
and by opening night,
you're putting it all at risk.

508
00:20:20,052 --> 00:20:22,763
Falling for your leading lady
is a rookie move.

509
00:20:22,804 --> 00:20:25,140
Trust me, showmances don't work.

510
00:20:27,017 --> 00:20:28,393
Are you talking about
you and Bea?

511
00:20:28,435 --> 00:20:29,853
What?

512
00:20:29,895 --> 00:20:31,688
No.
I'm talking generally,

513
00:20:31,730 --> 00:20:33,565
as a guy who's been around
a long time.

514
00:20:33,607 --> 00:20:35,484
Never works out.
Never.

515
00:20:36,985 --> 00:20:38,195
Okay, can we talk
about something

516
00:20:38,237 --> 00:20:39,446
other than Shakespeare
for a second?

517
00:20:39,488 --> 00:20:41,448
Not my strong suit, but sure.

518
00:20:41,490 --> 00:20:43,742
What's up with you and Benny?

519
00:20:43,784 --> 00:20:45,911
There's some heavy-duty
flirting going on.

520
00:20:45,953 --> 00:20:47,329
What?

521
00:20:47,371 --> 00:20:49,957
Are you insane?!
We're not flirting!

522
00:20:49,998 --> 00:20:52,793
We're... we're debating.
We're working through things.

523
00:20:52,834 --> 00:20:53,919
Could have fooled me!

524
00:20:53,961 --> 00:20:55,045
No, no, no, no.

525
00:20:55,087 --> 00:20:56,964
That's the way
that Benny and I are.

526
00:20:57,005 --> 00:21:00,467
It's... it's our process.
It's not romantic at all.

527
00:21:00,509 --> 00:21:02,761
- So, you're friends, then?
- Well, I wouldn't say that.

528
00:21:02,803 --> 00:21:03,804
- Enemies?
- Please.

529
00:21:03,845 --> 00:21:05,097
Bitter rivals.

530
00:21:05,138 --> 00:21:07,224
Hannah, it's...

531
00:21:07,266 --> 00:21:09,017
complicated.

532
00:21:09,059 --> 00:21:11,061
There is a history there.

533
00:21:11,103 --> 00:21:13,230
We... were together.

534
00:21:13,271 --> 00:21:15,232
- There it is!
- For a very short time!

535
00:21:15,273 --> 00:21:16,650
Why'd you break up?

536
00:21:16,692 --> 00:21:17,985
We just weren't a good match.

537
00:21:20,112 --> 00:21:23,031
So, no hard feelings,
no hearts broken,

538
00:21:23,073 --> 00:21:25,909
no lingering feelings
on either side at all?

539
00:21:25,951 --> 00:21:28,120
Do you know what, it was
such a long time ago,

540
00:21:28,161 --> 00:21:29,746
and... and we've moved on;

541
00:21:29,788 --> 00:21:32,124
we've both had other
relationships, and...

542
00:21:32,165 --> 00:21:34,543
now it's just like
we're on the same plane.

543
00:21:34,584 --> 00:21:35,711
You know?

544
00:21:35,752 --> 00:21:37,421
We just have a shorthand.

545
00:21:37,462 --> 00:21:39,965
And it's not a romantic plane?

546
00:21:40,007 --> 00:21:42,467
No. I-I find him
incredibly irritating.

547
00:21:44,219 --> 00:21:46,221
Well, that's too bad.

548
00:21:46,263 --> 00:21:47,681
I was hoping
we could double-date.

549
00:21:47,723 --> 00:21:49,558
Double-date?
Who are you dating?

550
00:21:49,599 --> 00:21:51,351
No, I dunno.
I mean theoretically.

551
00:21:54,771 --> 00:21:56,648
So, how's it goin'
with Petruchio?

552
00:21:56,690 --> 00:21:59,526
Oh, I am totally lost right now.

553
00:21:59,568 --> 00:22:00,819
Kate and Petruchio's
relationship

554
00:22:00,861 --> 00:22:02,696
doesn't make any sense.

555
00:22:02,738 --> 00:22:03,989
They fight like crazy,

556
00:22:04,031 --> 00:22:05,490
but in an affectionate
kinda way.

557
00:22:05,532 --> 00:22:07,409
I know.
It's complex.

558
00:22:07,451 --> 00:22:09,327
Ask your director.

559
00:22:09,369 --> 00:22:12,581
Yeah, but you got insight.
Can't you help me out?

560
00:22:12,622 --> 00:22:14,458
I'm just a fellow actor, Clay.

561
00:22:16,376 --> 00:22:19,004
You know,
there's this rumour going around

562
00:22:19,046 --> 00:22:22,674
that Toni might be retiring
as head of the festival.

563
00:22:22,716 --> 00:22:23,842
Where'd you hear that?

564
00:22:23,884 --> 00:22:25,927
It's a tight-knit group.
Word gets around.

565
00:22:25,969 --> 00:22:27,763
I assume Bea's taking over?

566
00:22:27,804 --> 00:22:30,640
She is.
Bea's brilliant.

567
00:22:30,682 --> 00:22:33,060
You know, capable,
a very imaginative director.

568
00:22:33,101 --> 00:22:35,062
- Mm-hm.
- And a leader.

569
00:22:35,103 --> 00:22:38,356
Although she does have
some impossible shoes to fill.

570
00:22:38,398 --> 00:22:41,735
Pretty brave to follow up
an icon like Toni Bishop,

571
00:22:41,777 --> 00:22:43,111
especially when she's your mom.

572
00:22:48,450 --> 00:22:52,120
Which I can fancy more
than any other.

573
00:22:52,162 --> 00:22:54,581
Minion, thou liest!

574
00:22:54,623 --> 00:22:55,874
Is't not...

575
00:22:57,751 --> 00:22:58,835
Hortensio.

576
00:22:58,877 --> 00:23:00,337
Oh!

577
00:23:00,378 --> 00:23:01,296
Okay.

578
00:23:04,925 --> 00:23:07,302
Hortensio, thou liest!
Is't not...

579
00:23:07,344 --> 00:23:08,386
Bianca...

580
00:23:09,971 --> 00:23:11,056
Sorry, you're Bianca.

581
00:23:11,098 --> 00:23:12,557
- Yeah.
- Okay, let's take five.

582
00:23:12,599 --> 00:23:13,683
Argh!

583
00:23:13,725 --> 00:23:15,143
Yeah, yeah.
Maggie, thank you.

584
00:23:17,771 --> 00:23:19,940
I'm so sorry, Bea.
It's just, there's so many names

585
00:23:19,981 --> 00:23:22,526
and it's just so much dialogue,
and the scenes are just so long.

586
00:23:22,567 --> 00:23:24,236
Do you remember
that scene that we shot

587
00:23:24,277 --> 00:23:26,279
on the hospital rooftop?
Um, it was that

588
00:23:26,321 --> 00:23:28,532
really long scene that you had
to memorize on the show?

589
00:23:28,573 --> 00:23:30,117
- Yeah.
- How did you do that?

590
00:23:31,785 --> 00:23:34,287
I don't know, I just
paced around until it stuck.

591
00:23:34,329 --> 00:23:35,831
Oh, that's perfect.

592
00:23:35,872 --> 00:23:38,416
Why don't you two
walk to the market

593
00:23:38,458 --> 00:23:40,460
and you can run lines?

594
00:23:40,502 --> 00:23:42,003
I know.
I'll go find Clay.

595
00:23:42,045 --> 00:23:44,297
Oh no, Maggie's here.
Maggie, are you free?

596
00:23:44,339 --> 00:23:45,674
Yeah, Maggie can do it.
Maggie's...

597
00:23:45,715 --> 00:23:46,925
Oh, he's already
texting me back.

598
00:23:46,967 --> 00:23:48,093
Oh.

599
00:23:48,135 --> 00:23:49,803
Thank you, Bea.

600
00:23:49,845 --> 00:23:52,806
I'll be back soon,
and I will know my lines.

601
00:23:52,848 --> 00:23:53,807
Okay.

602
00:23:55,058 --> 00:23:56,143
Perfect.

603
00:24:01,690 --> 00:24:05,152
The food market's at, you know,
1 o'clock on a Wednesday.

604
00:24:22,752 --> 00:24:23,962
Really good.
What's this one?

605
00:24:25,005 --> 00:24:27,007
Aah!
Oh!

606
00:24:27,048 --> 00:24:28,842
What are you doing here?

607
00:24:28,884 --> 00:24:29,926
- Same as you.
- Come on.

608
00:24:29,968 --> 00:24:31,428
I'm following the lovebirds.

609
00:24:31,470 --> 00:24:34,431
They're not lovebirds!
They're running lines.

610
00:24:34,472 --> 00:24:35,765
Then why are you following them?

611
00:24:35,807 --> 00:24:37,767
I wanna make sure
they stay focused.

612
00:24:37,809 --> 00:24:39,603
Hannah learns her lines best
when she's walking.

613
00:24:39,644 --> 00:24:41,563
Can you give me some space,
please?

614
00:24:42,981 --> 00:24:44,441
Maybe she's struggling with
Shakespeare's wording.

615
00:24:45,901 --> 00:24:47,903
It might help her if she
shortens the lines down

616
00:24:47,944 --> 00:24:49,404
to the first letter
in each word.

617
00:24:49,446 --> 00:24:51,156
And uses the acronym
to trigger her memory.

618
00:24:51,198 --> 00:24:52,782
Of course!
I forgot about that old trick.

619
00:24:52,824 --> 00:24:55,035
Good idea.

620
00:24:55,076 --> 00:24:57,579
Does it hurt your brain when
you're forced to compliment me?

621
00:24:59,581 --> 00:25:00,832
Luckily, it rarely happens.

622
00:25:03,001 --> 00:25:04,419
- Oh! They're heading this way!
- Gah!

623
00:25:17,182 --> 00:25:18,308
Benny...?

624
00:25:20,227 --> 00:25:22,354
Shh!

625
00:25:26,525 --> 00:25:28,026
It's the energy
from the audience

626
00:25:28,068 --> 00:25:29,569
that makes it so great,
don't you think?

627
00:25:29,611 --> 00:25:30,820
Be honest...

628
00:25:30,862 --> 00:25:32,197
- What are they doing?
- I can't see.

629
00:25:32,239 --> 00:25:33,281
You'll be fine.

630
00:25:33,323 --> 00:25:35,033
I have two weeks!
That's it.

631
00:25:35,075 --> 00:25:37,160
That's tons of time.

632
00:25:37,202 --> 00:25:39,621
I don't know if I can do this.

633
00:25:39,663 --> 00:25:40,914
I'll be here to help.

634
00:25:40,956 --> 00:25:42,040
Thank you.

635
00:25:43,959 --> 00:25:45,252
Like I said...

636
00:25:45,293 --> 00:25:46,545
lovebirds.

637
00:25:46,586 --> 00:25:48,838
Oh, no...
this is not good for my play!

638
00:25:48,880 --> 00:25:50,215
No, it's not.

639
00:25:52,092 --> 00:25:54,427
Okay, well, why are we hiding?
Why don't we go confront them?

640
00:25:54,469 --> 00:25:56,888
No, you can't just
ambush them out in public.

641
00:25:56,930 --> 00:25:58,598
I'll talk to Clay,
you talk to Hannah.

642
00:25:58,640 --> 00:26:00,016
And say what?

643
00:26:00,058 --> 00:26:02,060
Remind them that showmances
are a terrible idea.

644
00:26:02,102 --> 00:26:04,229
And they can completely
distract you from your work.

645
00:26:04,271 --> 00:26:06,314
And they always end
in a miserable break up.

646
00:26:08,733 --> 00:26:10,860
That doesn't always
have to happen.

647
00:26:10,902 --> 00:26:11,945
Oh, no?

648
00:26:13,905 --> 00:26:15,448
Can we stay focused
on the play, please?

649
00:26:16,700 --> 00:26:18,285
Yeah, yeah.
Um, yeah, fine by me.

650
00:26:18,326 --> 00:26:20,245
- Thank you.
- No, no, no...

651
00:26:20,287 --> 00:26:21,496
Thank <i>you.</i>

652
00:26:32,132 --> 00:26:35,969
Whoa, Maggie!
You look stunning in that dress.

653
00:26:37,220 --> 00:26:39,222
It's gorgeous, right?

654
00:26:39,264 --> 00:26:40,473
Where's Hannah?

655
00:26:40,515 --> 00:26:42,642
She's supposed to be
in fittings all morning.

656
00:26:42,684 --> 00:26:43,810
I'm here!

657
00:26:45,353 --> 00:26:46,271
And I brought coffee.

658
00:26:46,313 --> 00:26:47,564
You're late.

659
00:26:47,605 --> 00:26:49,899
I know, I had to jump
on a call with my agents.

660
00:26:49,941 --> 00:26:51,401
Since we announced
that I'm doing Shakespeare,

661
00:26:51,443 --> 00:26:53,028
some pretty cool projects
are coming my way

662
00:26:53,069 --> 00:26:54,946
that you'd be perfect to direct.

663
00:26:54,988 --> 00:26:56,906
Thank you. Not that that's
an excuse for being late.

664
00:26:56,948 --> 00:26:58,325
I'm sorry.

665
00:26:58,366 --> 00:27:00,910
That's really exciting.
Thank you for thinking of me.

666
00:27:00,952 --> 00:27:02,537
You got it, sister.

667
00:27:02,579 --> 00:27:05,081
Hannah, I actually... I need
to talk to you about something.

668
00:27:05,123 --> 00:27:06,958
Ladies, will you
just give us a moment?

669
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:10,545
Maggie,
I'm gonna need that dress back.

670
00:27:10,587 --> 00:27:12,631
- I was thinking I could keep it.
- No.

671
00:27:12,672 --> 00:27:14,549
- Okay. No, no.
- Okay.

672
00:27:14,591 --> 00:27:17,135
Oh, no.
Am I in trouble?

673
00:27:17,177 --> 00:27:18,803
It is about the play.

674
00:27:18,845 --> 00:27:21,014
You and Clay.

675
00:27:25,769 --> 00:27:27,479
We thought
we were hiding it well.

676
00:27:27,520 --> 00:27:29,105
You thought kissing
in the middle of the market

677
00:27:29,147 --> 00:27:30,357
was hiding it well?

678
00:27:30,398 --> 00:27:32,525
I can't help it!

679
00:27:32,567 --> 00:27:34,778
He's the nicest guy
I've ever met.

680
00:27:34,819 --> 00:27:37,822
He's so sweet,
and there's just this...

681
00:27:37,864 --> 00:27:39,324
electricity between us.

682
00:27:39,366 --> 00:27:40,659
I'm disappointing you.

683
00:27:40,700 --> 00:27:42,285
No...

684
00:27:42,327 --> 00:27:43,870
You can date whoever you want.

685
00:27:45,246 --> 00:27:46,956
It's not just dating.

686
00:27:48,458 --> 00:27:50,669
We're falling in love, Bea.

687
00:27:50,710 --> 00:27:53,380
You've only known each other
a couple of weeks!

688
00:27:53,421 --> 00:27:55,423
Plus, you're playing characters
that are falling in love.

689
00:27:55,465 --> 00:27:57,675
That's not real love, Hannah.

690
00:27:57,717 --> 00:27:59,552
That... that's called
festival love.

691
00:27:59,594 --> 00:28:01,054
No, it's not festival love, Bea.

692
00:28:01,096 --> 00:28:02,472
It's... <i>love</i> love.

693
00:28:06,059 --> 00:28:07,560
Okay, sit down.

694
00:28:07,602 --> 00:28:08,645
Come on.

695
00:28:16,194 --> 00:28:18,780
I've been in your shoes before.

696
00:28:18,822 --> 00:28:21,700
When Benny and I were doing
Romeo and Juliet, it was like...

697
00:28:23,284 --> 00:28:24,536
we were meant to be.

698
00:28:28,081 --> 00:28:31,084
I still don't understand what
happened between the two of you.

699
00:28:31,126 --> 00:28:32,293
It was a bad review.

700
00:28:33,920 --> 00:28:35,255
It killed me.

701
00:28:37,173 --> 00:28:39,175
"Bea Bishop is distracted

702
00:28:39,217 --> 00:28:42,554
"and uninspired
in the role of Juliet."

703
00:28:42,595 --> 00:28:43,888
I'm sorry,

704
00:28:43,930 --> 00:28:46,182
you have a bad review
from 20 years ago memorized?

705
00:28:46,224 --> 00:28:49,185
Well, I'm paraphrasing,
but the gist was I stunk.

706
00:28:49,227 --> 00:28:50,228
I doubt that.

707
00:28:51,730 --> 00:28:54,357
Why did that mean
you and Benny had to break up?

708
00:28:54,399 --> 00:28:56,192
Well, after the review came out,

709
00:28:56,234 --> 00:28:58,611
I decided
I never wanted to act again.

710
00:29:00,321 --> 00:29:02,657
Benny was this shooting star.

711
00:29:02,699 --> 00:29:04,117
I mean,
everyone knew he was gonna

712
00:29:04,159 --> 00:29:05,535
do incredible things
on the stage,

713
00:29:05,577 --> 00:29:07,996
just like my mother, who had
an amazing acting career,

714
00:29:08,037 --> 00:29:09,539
and then
she founded the festival,

715
00:29:09,581 --> 00:29:11,499
and I was just never
gonna measure up.

716
00:29:11,541 --> 00:29:12,667
What did Benny say?

717
00:29:16,045 --> 00:29:17,672
I never talked to him about it.

718
00:29:19,507 --> 00:29:22,135
I bumbled my way through
the rest of the run,

719
00:29:22,177 --> 00:29:23,678
I broke it off with him,

720
00:29:23,720 --> 00:29:25,555
and I started
studying directing.

721
00:29:25,597 --> 00:29:27,390
So, you still
haven't discussed it?

722
00:29:27,432 --> 00:29:29,601
I-I don't wanna revisit that.

723
00:29:29,642 --> 00:29:31,186
Scaredy-cat!

724
00:29:31,227 --> 00:29:33,563
Hey! I didn't even wanna
talk to <i>you</i> about it,

725
00:29:33,605 --> 00:29:36,357
except that I need you
to understand why I'm worried.

726
00:29:36,399 --> 00:29:38,902
Hannah, if you go out
on that stage

727
00:29:38,943 --> 00:29:41,237
and all you're thinking about
is how much you love Clay,

728
00:29:41,279 --> 00:29:43,907
the audience isn't gonna
see Kate's story.

729
00:29:43,948 --> 00:29:47,368
They're gonna see Hannah,
the TV actress who's...

730
00:29:47,410 --> 00:29:49,412
got a crush on her co-star.

731
00:29:49,454 --> 00:29:51,748
And I won't let that
kind of review happen to you.

732
00:29:53,625 --> 00:29:54,751
I won't.

733
00:29:55,960 --> 00:29:57,045
I get it.

734
00:30:00,673 --> 00:30:02,383
- You do?
- Yeah.

735
00:30:03,801 --> 00:30:05,678
You're gonna be
so good in this play.

736
00:30:08,223 --> 00:30:10,475
And this dress is amazing!

737
00:30:10,517 --> 00:30:13,353
Well, you believing in me
is all I need.

738
00:30:13,394 --> 00:30:14,312
Thanks, Bea.

739
00:30:22,904 --> 00:30:25,365
Aah!
Good work, good job.

740
00:30:25,406 --> 00:30:28,701
Alright, now we'll show you
a little bit of a death scene.

741
00:30:28,743 --> 00:30:29,953
Hi-yah...!

742
00:30:32,789 --> 00:30:36,709
- Hi-yah!
- Aahhh!

743
00:30:43,299 --> 00:30:46,344
Alright. Good job, guys.
Great job, great job.

744
00:30:46,386 --> 00:30:47,595
- Good job.
- Good job.

745
00:30:47,637 --> 00:30:49,055
- Thanks, everyone.
- Good job.

746
00:30:49,097 --> 00:30:50,265
Good job, everybody.

747
00:30:50,306 --> 00:30:51,849
Now, that was fun.

748
00:30:51,891 --> 00:30:53,560
Rehearsal, on the other hand,
different story.

749
00:30:55,270 --> 00:30:57,480
You still struggling
with your character?

750
00:30:57,522 --> 00:30:59,691
Yeah.
You know, I asked Bea about it,

751
00:30:59,732 --> 00:31:02,318
and she guided me through
in a really smart way, but...

752
00:31:03,862 --> 00:31:06,114
I still don't get why Petruchio
can't be honest

753
00:31:06,155 --> 00:31:08,116
about his feelings towards Kate.

754
00:31:08,157 --> 00:31:11,703
Well, there's no guarantee that
she feels the same way, right?

755
00:31:11,744 --> 00:31:13,413
It's risky.

756
00:31:13,454 --> 00:31:15,665
Isn't all love risk, though?

757
00:31:15,707 --> 00:31:18,251
Maybe Petruchio's
been hurt in the past.

758
00:31:18,293 --> 00:31:20,211
Maybe he's had a broken heart.

759
00:31:20,253 --> 00:31:21,921
Or maybe he's just an idiot

760
00:31:21,963 --> 00:31:23,464
and he needs
to get over himself.

761
00:31:23,506 --> 00:31:25,425
If he would just
be honest with her,

762
00:31:25,466 --> 00:31:27,885
maybe he wouldn't have to be
miserable all the time.

763
00:31:32,390 --> 00:31:34,058
Are we still
talking about Petruchio?

764
00:31:35,435 --> 00:31:38,354
Petruchio is... messy.

765
00:31:38,396 --> 00:31:40,940
Says the wrong thing,
makes bad choices.

766
00:31:40,982 --> 00:31:42,775
But like every good character,
he'll figure himself out

767
00:31:42,817 --> 00:31:44,360
in the end.

768
00:31:44,402 --> 00:31:46,904
Nail down where he is in
each scene along that journey,

769
00:31:46,946 --> 00:31:48,281
and you'll have it.
I promise.

770
00:31:49,782 --> 00:31:51,910
- You make it so simple.
- Mm-hm.

771
00:31:51,951 --> 00:31:53,578
You're overthinking it.

772
00:31:53,620 --> 00:31:54,621
Thanks, man.

773
00:31:54,662 --> 00:31:55,955
Yeah.

774
00:31:55,997 --> 00:31:57,290
It's just
a little friendly advice.

775
00:31:59,125 --> 00:32:00,710
And speaking of advice...

776
00:32:04,213 --> 00:32:06,674
you and Hannah are a thing, huh?

777
00:32:06,716 --> 00:32:08,926
Don't bother trying to deny it.

778
00:32:08,968 --> 00:32:10,637
When you spend all day
in rehearsal

779
00:32:10,678 --> 00:32:12,472
convincing your mind that
you're in love with someone,

780
00:32:12,513 --> 00:32:14,098
it's natural that
you fall for them.

781
00:32:14,140 --> 00:32:16,768
But it's festival love.

782
00:32:18,478 --> 00:32:20,563
I don't know.

783
00:32:20,605 --> 00:32:22,774
I've never felt
this way about...

784
00:32:22,815 --> 00:32:24,692
anyone before.

785
00:32:24,734 --> 00:32:28,154
Steel yourself, Sir Clay!

786
00:32:28,196 --> 00:32:30,490
The last thing you want
is a broken heart, buddy.

787
00:32:30,531 --> 00:32:31,491
Alright?

788
00:32:31,532 --> 00:32:32,617
Yeah.

789
00:32:35,161 --> 00:32:36,079
Good work.

790
00:32:38,039 --> 00:32:39,123
Oof!

791
00:32:39,165 --> 00:32:40,750
You're gonna have
to do better than that!

792
00:32:40,792 --> 00:32:42,126
I haven't played
this in forever.

793
00:32:42,168 --> 00:32:44,212
Oh, no, you were supposed
to be our secret ringer.

794
00:32:44,253 --> 00:32:46,464
You've really become a big
part of this event, haven't you?

795
00:32:46,506 --> 00:32:48,466
I think it's important
to give back to the community

796
00:32:48,508 --> 00:32:49,842
that supports us.

797
00:32:49,884 --> 00:32:51,636
I think you've gotten
really good at getting people

798
00:32:51,678 --> 00:32:52,971
excited about theatre.

799
00:32:53,012 --> 00:32:54,013
Hmph.

800
00:32:56,099 --> 00:32:57,517
Blam!
See that?

801
00:32:57,558 --> 00:32:58,851
That's how you do it.

802
00:32:58,893 --> 00:33:01,187
- Sorry, I blinked.
- Oh.

803
00:33:01,229 --> 00:33:03,189
Have you ever thought about
becoming a teacher, Benny?

804
00:33:03,231 --> 00:33:04,732
Back in the day.

805
00:33:04,774 --> 00:33:07,235
Luckily for me,
the acting thing worked out.

806
00:33:07,276 --> 00:33:09,237
Those who can't do, teach.
Am I right?

807
00:33:09,278 --> 00:33:11,990
No, those who have
put in years of hard work

808
00:33:12,031 --> 00:33:13,700
and earned the respect
of their peers...

809
00:33:13,741 --> 00:33:15,284
they teach.

810
00:33:15,326 --> 00:33:17,912
Passing knowledge on to a future
generation is a privilege.

811
00:33:17,954 --> 00:33:19,330
What are you doing?

812
00:33:19,372 --> 00:33:20,999
You've completely
locked out your elbows.

813
00:33:21,040 --> 00:33:22,750
- No, I haven't.
- Okay, just...

814
00:33:24,210 --> 00:33:26,713
just, um... just loosen
your stance a little bit.

815
00:33:28,631 --> 00:33:29,757
Loosen your stance.
Loosen your hips.

816
00:33:29,799 --> 00:33:32,719
- Okay.
- Okay? Just...

817
00:33:34,387 --> 00:33:35,722
Sorry.

818
00:33:35,763 --> 00:33:37,432
Okay.
There you go, okay.

819
00:33:37,473 --> 00:33:38,808
Just...

820
00:33:38,850 --> 00:33:40,059
take it easy, okay?

821
00:33:40,101 --> 00:33:41,185
Okay.

822
00:33:41,227 --> 00:33:42,562
Try again.

823
00:33:42,603 --> 00:33:43,521
Okay.

824
00:33:47,150 --> 00:33:48,359
Yikes!

825
00:33:48,401 --> 00:33:51,738
I knew that wasn't gonna work.

826
00:33:51,779 --> 00:33:53,948
Hey, how did it go with Clay?

827
00:33:53,990 --> 00:33:55,992
Ah, we're good. He gets it.
Hannah?

828
00:33:56,034 --> 00:33:58,244
Yeah, she gets it.

829
00:33:58,286 --> 00:34:00,121
By the way, I think
you should teach everybody

830
00:34:00,163 --> 00:34:01,205
that memorization method...

831
00:34:01,247 --> 00:34:03,458
the acronym one.

832
00:34:03,499 --> 00:34:04,917
Nah, you should teach 'em.

833
00:34:04,959 --> 00:34:08,129
No, you teach them.
Everybody looks up to you.

834
00:34:08,171 --> 00:34:10,506
- They do?
- Yeah, of course.

835
00:34:10,548 --> 00:34:12,925
I can't give
fellow actors a note.

836
00:34:12,967 --> 00:34:14,552
You're the director.

837
00:34:14,594 --> 00:34:17,013
Ah, sometimes actors connect
with each other differently.

838
00:34:17,054 --> 00:34:19,015
Well, you were an actor
once upon a time.

839
00:34:21,809 --> 00:34:24,854
A distracted and uninspired one.

840
00:34:26,439 --> 00:34:28,274
What are you talking about?

841
00:34:28,316 --> 00:34:29,650
The review...?

842
00:34:29,692 --> 00:34:30,902
From Romeo and Juliet?

843
00:34:30,943 --> 00:34:33,529
Ah, reviews are just noise.

844
00:34:33,571 --> 00:34:35,948
Well, that review took me down.

845
00:34:35,990 --> 00:34:38,326
Made me quit acting.
You don't remember that?

846
00:34:38,367 --> 00:34:40,870
I remember you leaving.

847
00:34:40,912 --> 00:34:42,997
Is it time...
for the great rematch?

848
00:34:44,665 --> 00:34:47,001
I can see that you're ready.
Alright, get into position.

849
00:34:47,043 --> 00:34:48,211
Oh...!

850
00:34:48,252 --> 00:34:49,754
This is gonna be
one for the ages.

851
00:34:49,796 --> 00:34:50,838
Come on, Bea.

852
00:34:50,880 --> 00:34:52,340
Is everybody warmed up?

853
00:34:52,381 --> 00:34:53,841
Should we stretch?

854
00:34:53,883 --> 00:34:55,343
Alright, you're up first.

855
00:34:56,969 --> 00:34:58,513
So, the masquerade party is a...

856
00:34:58,554 --> 00:35:01,057
tradition to celebrate
opening night?

857
00:35:01,099 --> 00:35:02,683
A few days
before the final show opens,

858
00:35:02,725 --> 00:35:05,103
we have this
amazing costume party.

859
00:35:05,144 --> 00:35:06,521
It's the best.

860
00:35:06,562 --> 00:35:08,564
My mask looks... unwell.

861
00:35:11,609 --> 00:35:13,528
Well, I think I'm kind of
nailing it over here.

862
00:35:13,569 --> 00:35:15,112
Yeah, you are.
Can I try it on?

863
00:35:15,154 --> 00:35:16,280
No, it's not dry.

864
00:35:16,322 --> 00:35:17,573
We're done.

865
00:35:17,615 --> 00:35:19,408
I know, I was just
paying you a compliment.

866
00:35:19,450 --> 00:35:20,785
I don't need
compliments right now.

867
00:35:20,827 --> 00:35:21,911
What's up with you two?

868
00:35:21,953 --> 00:35:23,538
You got this real...

869
00:35:23,579 --> 00:35:25,706
Bea and Benny thing
going on right now.

870
00:35:25,748 --> 00:35:27,583
What? No.
No, we do not.

871
00:35:27,625 --> 00:35:30,294
Hannah thinks Bea and Benny
are star-crossed lovers.

872
00:35:30,336 --> 00:35:32,755
Hannah, you're new here,
so let me explain something.

873
00:35:32,797 --> 00:35:34,799
The only thing
that Bea and Benny do

874
00:35:34,841 --> 00:35:36,634
when they're together is fight.

875
00:35:36,676 --> 00:35:38,761
I promise you, what's happening
with Bea and Benny is love.

876
00:35:38,803 --> 00:35:40,847
Weird, witty, passionate love.

877
00:35:40,888 --> 00:35:42,306
I mean, look at them.

878
00:35:42,348 --> 00:35:44,350
That's nothing but love.

879
00:35:44,392 --> 00:35:46,644
Even if Bea and Benny's
non-traditional chemistry

880
00:35:46,686 --> 00:35:48,521
is obvious to us,
they'll never see it.

881
00:35:48,563 --> 00:35:49,438
They're too stubborn.

882
00:35:50,857 --> 00:35:52,483
They need a little...

883
00:35:52,525 --> 00:35:53,943
push.

884
00:35:53,985 --> 00:35:55,778
We need to get them
out of their own way.

885
00:35:55,820 --> 00:35:57,071
How?

886
00:35:57,113 --> 00:35:58,739
The only way Bea
will feel safe enough

887
00:35:58,781 --> 00:35:59,907
to allow her feelings
for Benny...

888
00:35:59,949 --> 00:36:01,200
would be to prove...

889
00:36:01,242 --> 00:36:03,953
that Benny is head over heels
in love with her.

890
00:36:03,995 --> 00:36:05,997
But Benny would also need
that same proof

891
00:36:06,038 --> 00:36:08,749
in order to even admit
his feelings for her.

892
00:36:08,791 --> 00:36:11,043
Exactly.

893
00:36:11,085 --> 00:36:13,588
So... they may need
a little help

894
00:36:13,629 --> 00:36:15,798
from their friends?

895
00:36:15,840 --> 00:36:18,342
Uh-huh.

896
00:36:18,384 --> 00:36:20,678
Now this is getting really good.

897
00:36:20,720 --> 00:36:21,721
Right?

898
00:36:21,762 --> 00:36:22,889
Yeah.

899
00:36:32,106 --> 00:36:33,482
Incoming!
Bea. Incoming.

900
00:36:39,947 --> 00:36:41,240
I love female directors.

901
00:36:44,785 --> 00:36:46,370
He's obsessed with her.

902
00:36:46,412 --> 00:36:49,665
The only thing
that he focuses on is Bea.

903
00:36:49,707 --> 00:36:53,044
Going on and on about
her... her confidence

904
00:36:53,085 --> 00:36:54,503
and... and fearlessness.

905
00:36:55,796 --> 00:36:58,257
Have you heard him swoon
about her... hair?

906
00:36:58,299 --> 00:37:00,801
Her...

907
00:37:00,843 --> 00:37:03,095
gorgeous, speckled brunette...

908
00:37:06,724 --> 00:37:08,684
Uh, not to mention her...

909
00:37:08,726 --> 00:37:10,269
perfect posture.

910
00:37:10,311 --> 00:37:11,687
Yeah!

911
00:37:11,729 --> 00:37:14,190
If you're in love
with someone, just tell them.

912
00:37:14,231 --> 00:37:17,568
Benny's terrified of
what we're all terrified of...

913
00:37:17,610 --> 00:37:19,028
unrequited love.

914
00:37:19,070 --> 00:37:22,323
He's got a notebook filled
with poems about her.

915
00:37:22,365 --> 00:37:23,991
He swore me to secrecy.

916
00:37:24,033 --> 00:37:25,701
If she rejected him,

917
00:37:25,743 --> 00:37:27,203
I think that'd break him
in half.

918
00:37:29,789 --> 00:37:31,248
Oh!
Me!

919
00:37:31,290 --> 00:37:32,959
Hello, boys!

920
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:33,960
Oh...

921
00:37:36,045 --> 00:37:37,338
Hey, Bea.

922
00:37:37,380 --> 00:37:38,464
Hi!

923
00:37:38,506 --> 00:37:40,925
- What do you think?
- I'm confused.

924
00:37:40,967 --> 00:37:42,176
The costumes are confusing?

925
00:37:42,218 --> 00:37:44,095
Oh.
No, it... it's a...

926
00:37:44,136 --> 00:37:46,222
work in progress.

927
00:37:46,264 --> 00:37:48,724
So, should we go back
to rehearsal, then?

928
00:37:48,766 --> 00:37:49,976
Yes.

929
00:37:50,017 --> 00:37:51,143
Good.

930
00:37:52,395 --> 00:37:54,772
I'll get... I just need
one minute to myself.

931
00:37:54,814 --> 00:37:57,024
Will you just tell Doug Berrie
I'm on my way?

932
00:37:57,066 --> 00:37:59,110
But don't stress him out.
Tell him I'm like one minute.

933
00:37:59,151 --> 00:38:00,152
60 seconds.

934
00:38:00,194 --> 00:38:01,529
- Yeah.
- Yeah, sure thing.

935
00:38:01,570 --> 00:38:02,655
- Good.
- Great. Thank you.

936
00:38:02,697 --> 00:38:03,990
Awesome.

937
00:38:04,031 --> 00:38:06,075
- See ya out there, boss.
- Okay, boss!

938
00:38:14,041 --> 00:38:15,251
Whoa...

939
00:38:17,420 --> 00:38:19,422
Why are our bodies...

940
00:38:19,463 --> 00:38:21,215
weak... and smooth?

941
00:38:22,717 --> 00:38:24,802
Soft and weak.

942
00:38:24,844 --> 00:38:27,430
Ah, I promised Bea I would
have my lines memorized by now!

943
00:38:28,681 --> 00:38:30,683
Where is he?

944
00:38:30,725 --> 00:38:33,686
Doug Berrie said this is Benny's
usual treadmill time.

945
00:38:33,728 --> 00:38:35,229
- Oh!
- Benny, hey.

946
00:38:36,689 --> 00:38:38,357
Act natural.

947
00:38:38,399 --> 00:38:40,067
The... the...
a sauna is the...

948
00:38:40,109 --> 00:38:41,485
is the key to brain health.

949
00:38:42,987 --> 00:38:45,406
Yeah, I saw a video about that.
Yeah, actually.

950
00:38:46,824 --> 00:38:48,951
Benny's listening to music,
right? He can't hear us?

951
00:38:50,369 --> 00:38:53,039
No, he's got his headphones in.
You can keep going.

952
00:38:53,080 --> 00:38:54,874
As I was saying...

953
00:38:54,915 --> 00:38:57,793
it's sad that he has no idea
about her true feelings for him.

954
00:38:57,835 --> 00:38:59,295
You really think
he doesn't know?

955
00:38:59,336 --> 00:39:01,338
I mean, if I were him,
I would've figured it out

956
00:39:01,380 --> 00:39:02,506
a long time ago.

957
00:39:02,548 --> 00:39:04,550
But... pfft!
You know Benny.

958
00:39:04,592 --> 00:39:06,469
So... what did she say?

959
00:39:07,720 --> 00:39:08,721
Benny?

960
00:39:10,306 --> 00:39:12,058
- Benny!
- Benny!

961
00:39:13,309 --> 00:39:16,270
Benny...
Benny...

962
00:39:16,312 --> 00:39:19,065
Okay.
He's not listening.

963
00:39:19,106 --> 00:39:20,816
There's never been
anyone like him.

964
00:39:20,858 --> 00:39:23,194
And not only is he brilliant...

965
00:39:23,235 --> 00:39:25,071
but also hot.

966
00:39:25,112 --> 00:39:26,655
Mm-hm.

967
00:39:26,697 --> 00:39:28,741
Oh, and that he's
in incredible shape.

968
00:39:28,783 --> 00:39:30,534
I believe the phrase
she used was,

969
00:39:30,576 --> 00:39:31,744
"Looks great in a T-shirt."

970
00:39:34,121 --> 00:39:36,749
Uh... uh, so,
why doesn't she just tell him?

971
00:39:36,791 --> 00:39:39,335
Well, she's been writing
and rewriting this letter to him

972
00:39:39,376 --> 00:39:42,046
for the past year,
confessing her true feelings.

973
00:39:42,088 --> 00:39:44,048
But every time she finishes
a version, she just deletes it.

974
00:39:44,090 --> 00:39:45,341
That's crazy.

975
00:39:45,382 --> 00:39:47,218
It's like,
just kiss him already!

976
00:39:49,011 --> 00:39:52,848
Well, what are you gonna do?
We just gotta let Bea be Bea.

977
00:39:59,563 --> 00:40:01,941
Um, I gotta shower
before rehearsal,

978
00:40:01,982 --> 00:40:03,359
so, uh, let's get outta here.

979
00:40:04,819 --> 00:40:05,736
Bye, Benny.

980
00:40:17,414 --> 00:40:19,375
Can I get a sleeve, too, for it?

981
00:40:19,416 --> 00:40:21,335
Thank you, thank you, thank you.

982
00:40:21,377 --> 00:40:23,003
Fancy meeting you here.

983
00:40:23,045 --> 00:40:25,214
Yeah.
Love coffee.

984
00:40:25,256 --> 00:40:26,841
Were you, uh...

985
00:40:26,882 --> 00:40:28,300
hoping to find me here?

986
00:40:28,342 --> 00:40:31,637
What? Me? No, no.
I-I need fuel for rehearsal.

987
00:40:31,679 --> 00:40:34,056
Yes, yes.
Mainline that caffeine!

988
00:40:34,098 --> 00:40:36,058
- Ooh, boy!
- Boo-oy!

989
00:40:39,436 --> 00:40:40,896
- Should we head over?
- Yup.

990
00:40:42,648 --> 00:40:44,233
I just need
to ask you something.

991
00:40:44,275 --> 00:40:46,026
Something?

992
00:40:46,068 --> 00:40:47,069
What something is that?

993
00:40:49,572 --> 00:40:51,198
It's about the play.

994
00:40:51,240 --> 00:40:52,575
- The play?
- Yeah.

995
00:40:52,616 --> 00:40:54,827
You wanted to check in
with me about the play?

996
00:40:54,869 --> 00:40:56,203
Yeah.

997
00:40:56,245 --> 00:40:57,913
I think the thing
I'm struggling with

998
00:40:57,955 --> 00:40:59,582
is that everything
these two characters do

999
00:40:59,623 --> 00:41:01,292
doesn't line up with them
loving each other.

1000
00:41:01,333 --> 00:41:02,418
That's what the story is.

1001
00:41:02,460 --> 00:41:03,919
But is that really
the best version?

1002
00:41:03,961 --> 00:41:06,005
Because, to me,
if they were on the same side,

1003
00:41:06,046 --> 00:41:07,548
it'd be a way better story.

1004
00:41:07,590 --> 00:41:09,467
- You mean if they were a team?
- Yes.

1005
00:41:12,595 --> 00:41:14,388
I'm talking about
Kate and Petruchio.

1006
00:41:14,430 --> 00:41:15,514
- Right.
- Yeah.

1007
00:41:17,224 --> 00:41:19,018
How would that work?

1008
00:41:19,059 --> 00:41:20,561
Well, you'll hate this...

1009
00:41:20,603 --> 00:41:22,438
because it's an untraditional
take on a traditional play.

1010
00:41:22,480 --> 00:41:24,565
Oh, you wanna set it
on the moon now?

1011
00:41:24,607 --> 00:41:26,233
It's a little late
in the rehearsal process

1012
00:41:26,275 --> 00:41:28,527
- to fit the cast in spacesuits.
- Stop.

1013
00:41:28,569 --> 00:41:31,363
What if, instead of Kate being
forced to marry Petruchio...

1014
00:41:31,405 --> 00:41:33,949
she's just in it for the dowry?

1015
00:41:33,991 --> 00:41:35,701
Say more.

1016
00:41:35,743 --> 00:41:38,204
She's convinced her father
that she's being compliant,

1017
00:41:38,245 --> 00:41:40,539
- but that's her strategy.
- Why would she do that?

1018
00:41:40,581 --> 00:41:42,917
Because she and Petruchio
are scheming together.

1019
00:41:42,958 --> 00:41:44,668
And then that's how
they fall in love,

1020
00:41:44,710 --> 00:41:46,921
and then they get the money,
and then they outwit everybody.

1021
00:41:46,962 --> 00:41:48,297
Does the text
support it, though?

1022
00:41:48,339 --> 00:41:50,549
In spots, yes.

1023
00:41:50,591 --> 00:41:52,968
Unless you split
the dialogue up between them.

1024
00:41:53,010 --> 00:41:55,262
- Double up some lines.
- Exactly.

1025
00:41:55,304 --> 00:41:57,556
Have them said by both of them.

1026
00:41:57,598 --> 00:41:59,934
That way, it's all still
Mr. Shakespeare's words, but...

1027
00:41:59,975 --> 00:42:01,936
You're just using
them differently.

1028
00:42:01,977 --> 00:42:03,896
Purists will be mad.
Heck, you'll be mad.

1029
00:42:03,938 --> 00:42:05,064
You'll be madder than anyone.

1030
00:42:05,105 --> 00:42:07,107
Oh, you know every
alarm bell in my body

1031
00:42:07,149 --> 00:42:08,192
is clanging like crazy.

1032
00:42:09,735 --> 00:42:12,780
But I also know if anyone
can pull it off, it's you.

1033
00:42:14,740 --> 00:42:16,825
You really mean that?

1034
00:42:16,867 --> 00:42:18,202
You know I do.

1035
00:42:22,790 --> 00:42:24,416
What about the board?

1036
00:42:24,458 --> 00:42:26,794
If it doesn't work, they won't
approve me as festival head.

1037
00:42:26,835 --> 00:42:28,337
They will,
if the audience loves it.

1038
00:42:28,379 --> 00:42:30,214
And this is a solid way
to do that.

1039
00:42:30,256 --> 00:42:32,299
I can't believe I was gonna
make the whole play

1040
00:42:32,341 --> 00:42:33,550
about battling stockbrokers.

1041
00:42:33,592 --> 00:42:35,552
I know.
I nearly passed out.

1042
00:42:35,594 --> 00:42:37,721
It's what you said about
historical context, right?

1043
00:42:37,763 --> 00:42:41,058
So, we'll do, in addition
to the hybrid costumes,

1044
00:42:41,100 --> 00:42:43,227
we'll have them
share dialogue as well.

1045
00:42:43,269 --> 00:42:46,105
And if you adjust the lines
just a bit,

1046
00:42:46,146 --> 00:42:49,233
this can become a love story
that everyone relates to.

1047
00:42:49,275 --> 00:42:51,402
I can't believe you just
said those words out loud.

1048
00:42:51,443 --> 00:42:53,195
Me neither.

1049
00:42:53,237 --> 00:42:55,698
Are we having
a creative conversation?

1050
00:42:55,739 --> 00:42:57,116
We are.

1051
00:42:57,157 --> 00:42:58,409
Without fighting?

1052
00:42:58,450 --> 00:42:59,577
Yes, ma'am.

1053
00:42:59,618 --> 00:43:01,078
For the first time ever?

1054
00:43:03,205 --> 00:43:04,915
There is something
I wanna ask you.

1055
00:43:06,250 --> 00:43:08,043
There's something
I wanna ask you too.

1056
00:43:09,503 --> 00:43:11,463
Are we rehearsing
this thing or what?

1057
00:43:11,505 --> 00:43:12,673
We are burning daylight.

1058
00:43:12,715 --> 00:43:14,216
Time's a-tickin',
my pretty chickens.

1059
00:43:14,258 --> 00:43:16,176
Yes, Doug,
we're right behind you.

1060
00:43:18,012 --> 00:43:18,971
<i>Après</i> thee.

1061
00:43:21,140 --> 00:43:22,182
Right in front of you.

1062
00:43:25,352 --> 00:43:26,937
- Is everyone else...?
- Yeah.

1063
00:44:16,320 --> 00:44:20,324
- One, two, three!
- Shakespeare!

1064
00:44:20,366 --> 00:44:22,201
Ha-ha!
We got this.

1065
00:44:22,242 --> 00:44:23,744
- We do.
- I love this.

1066
00:44:28,415 --> 00:44:29,375
Okay.

1067
00:44:33,379 --> 00:44:35,672
I care not.

1068
00:44:35,714 --> 00:44:38,550
Nay, hear you, Kate:
in sooth, you 'scape not so.

1069
00:44:38,592 --> 00:44:40,552
I'm so sorry, just,
can we go one more time?

1070
00:44:43,472 --> 00:44:45,182
- You ready?
- Yeah.

1071
00:44:48,394 --> 00:44:49,812
I care not.

1072
00:44:49,853 --> 00:44:51,647
Nay, hear you, Kate:
in sooth, you 'scape not so.

1073
00:44:58,946 --> 00:45:00,114
Um...

1074
00:45:00,155 --> 00:45:01,532
Uh... uh!

1075
00:45:01,573 --> 00:45:03,659
I chafe you if I tarry.

1076
00:45:03,701 --> 00:45:05,327
Let me go.

1077
00:45:05,369 --> 00:45:06,245
No, not a whit.

1078
00:45:08,122 --> 00:45:10,958
- What are you doing?
- Low blood sugar, I guess.

1079
00:45:10,999 --> 00:45:12,793
I'm trying to concentrate.

1080
00:45:12,835 --> 00:45:14,378
Because we open
in less than a week,

1081
00:45:14,420 --> 00:45:15,462
and you're having a snack.

1082
00:45:15,504 --> 00:45:17,047
I'm just deep in the character.

1083
00:45:17,089 --> 00:45:19,091
Right, you're
the seasoned theatre actor

1084
00:45:19,133 --> 00:45:21,385
and I'm just a TV person who
doesn't know what she's doing?

1085
00:45:21,427 --> 00:45:24,263
Okay.
Let's take a breath. Um...

1086
00:45:24,304 --> 00:45:25,931
This is not easy, right?

1087
00:45:25,973 --> 00:45:27,599
So, let's take it from...
Hannah?

1088
00:45:28,934 --> 00:45:30,811
Hannah?

1089
00:45:30,853 --> 00:45:32,020
I'll go after her...

1090
00:45:32,062 --> 00:45:33,480
so you can keep
going with everyone else.

1091
00:45:33,522 --> 00:45:34,440
Thank you, Benny.

1092
00:45:37,276 --> 00:45:38,193
Really?

1093
00:45:40,654 --> 00:45:41,697
Hey, there.

1094
00:45:43,031 --> 00:45:44,241
Hannah?

1095
00:45:45,742 --> 00:45:47,035
Are you okay?

1096
00:45:47,077 --> 00:45:48,787
Yeah. Yeah.
Totally okay.

1097
00:45:53,667 --> 00:45:54,751
Have you ever felt...

1098
00:45:56,462 --> 00:45:58,046
truly terrible at your job?

1099
00:45:59,131 --> 00:46:00,340
Sure.

1100
00:46:00,382 --> 00:46:03,302
Years ago, Toni,
she got some funding

1101
00:46:03,343 --> 00:46:05,387
to train a handful of actors.

1102
00:46:05,429 --> 00:46:08,557
I applied, I got in,
and I moved here to study.

1103
00:46:08,599 --> 00:46:10,642
I was the only actor
who came from out of town.

1104
00:46:11,810 --> 00:46:14,021
It was terrifying.

1105
00:46:14,062 --> 00:46:17,775
And back then,
I think I truly was terrible,

1106
00:46:17,816 --> 00:46:18,901
as you phrased it.

1107
00:46:21,695 --> 00:46:24,072
How did you get over that?

1108
00:46:25,282 --> 00:46:26,992
Bea was in that workshop too.

1109
00:46:29,119 --> 00:46:31,163
We were kids.

1110
00:46:31,205 --> 00:46:33,081
The first day, we were
put together in a scene

1111
00:46:33,123 --> 00:46:36,001
as Rosencrantz and Guildenstern.

1112
00:46:36,043 --> 00:46:38,045
Not the most romantic
of pairings.

1113
00:46:39,087 --> 00:46:40,297
No.

1114
00:46:40,339 --> 00:46:41,256
Not at all.

1115
00:46:43,759 --> 00:46:44,760
But we crushed it.

1116
00:46:46,720 --> 00:46:48,972
After that, I stopped
worrying about if I was good.

1117
00:46:50,557 --> 00:46:52,935
I just wanted
to show up for her.

1118
00:46:52,976 --> 00:46:54,019
She was fearless.

1119
00:46:55,687 --> 00:46:57,606
Patient.

1120
00:46:57,648 --> 00:47:00,192
She had
this playfulness about her.

1121
00:47:00,234 --> 00:47:01,151
It freed me.

1122
00:47:02,528 --> 00:47:03,403
It's still there.

1123
00:47:07,950 --> 00:47:10,035
I wanna crush it
like that with Clay.

1124
00:47:10,077 --> 00:47:11,870
Good, 'cause there's still time.

1125
00:47:14,456 --> 00:47:16,166
Thank you
for talking to me, Benny.

1126
00:47:16,208 --> 00:47:18,335
Mm-hm.

1127
00:47:18,377 --> 00:47:21,004
Now I just need to figure out
how to get these blasted lines

1128
00:47:21,046 --> 00:47:22,631
into my brain.

1129
00:47:22,673 --> 00:47:25,008
I don't think the acronym trick
is working for me.

1130
00:47:26,635 --> 00:47:28,971
I have another method that
might help with memorization.

1131
00:47:29,012 --> 00:47:30,430
It's pretty easy
once you learn it.

1132
00:47:30,472 --> 00:47:32,266
Okay.
Really?

1133
00:47:32,307 --> 00:47:34,977
Yeah.
Yeah, let me teach it to you.

1134
00:47:35,018 --> 00:47:36,895
Have you heard of
a memory palace?

1135
00:47:36,937 --> 00:47:38,438
Yup.

1136
00:47:38,480 --> 00:47:41,316
It's not nearly as complicated
as that, don't worry.

1137
00:47:43,860 --> 00:47:45,696
- Try that, but...
- It's making her work for it.

1138
00:47:45,737 --> 00:47:46,905
- Right!
- Basically.

1139
00:47:46,947 --> 00:47:48,865
Right, make him earn it.
Just play with it.

1140
00:47:48,907 --> 00:47:51,660
Mom! Juan! Ah!

1141
00:47:51,702 --> 00:47:53,245
What brings you by?

1142
00:47:53,287 --> 00:47:56,248
We were just going over
some financials for next season.

1143
00:47:56,290 --> 00:47:58,375
Is Hannah Michaels
not rehearsing today?

1144
00:47:58,417 --> 00:48:00,335
Oh, she's just getting some air.

1145
00:48:00,377 --> 00:48:02,379
Awfully confident of her
to skip rehearsal.

1146
00:48:02,421 --> 00:48:03,380
And where's Clay?

1147
00:48:04,631 --> 00:48:07,175
He's getting some...
other air.

1148
00:48:07,217 --> 00:48:08,343
Really?

1149
00:48:08,385 --> 00:48:10,304
And in the meantime
we're sorting out

1150
00:48:10,345 --> 00:48:11,972
some of the blocking
with Don and Maggie.

1151
00:48:12,014 --> 00:48:13,015
Have you met Maggie?

1152
00:48:13,056 --> 00:48:16,310
Juan, come here!

1153
00:48:16,351 --> 00:48:19,271
Maggie!
You two will love each other.

1154
00:48:19,313 --> 00:48:20,856
Yeah, ask her about Ashland.

1155
00:48:22,524 --> 00:48:25,152
Is anything wrong?

1156
00:48:25,193 --> 00:48:26,903
My two leads aren't
speaking to each other,

1157
00:48:26,945 --> 00:48:28,488
and if I don't
pull off this play

1158
00:48:28,530 --> 00:48:29,781
to critical
and financial success,

1159
00:48:29,823 --> 00:48:31,366
then I won't get my dream job

1160
00:48:31,408 --> 00:48:33,368
and then the whole thing
gets sold off for parts

1161
00:48:33,410 --> 00:48:34,995
and then I leave your legacy
in tatters.

1162
00:48:35,037 --> 00:48:36,413
But other than that,
no, nothing's wrong.

1163
00:48:40,042 --> 00:48:42,753
That was dramatic.

1164
00:48:42,794 --> 00:48:44,046
Where's Benny?

1165
00:48:44,087 --> 00:48:46,298
He usually brings out
the best in you.

1166
00:48:46,340 --> 00:48:48,925
You know, he actually might be
the reason I'm spiralling.

1167
00:48:48,967 --> 00:48:50,427
Ooh.

1168
00:48:50,469 --> 00:48:51,345
Let me guess.

1169
00:48:52,638 --> 00:48:53,972
You've realized

1170
00:48:54,014 --> 00:48:56,433
that there's still
something special between you?

1171
00:48:56,475 --> 00:48:58,894
How am I the very
last person to know about this?

1172
00:48:58,936 --> 00:49:01,313
Oh, I hope they didn't
give you any spoilers.

1173
00:49:01,355 --> 00:49:03,482
It's a 400-year-old play;
I already know the ending.

1174
00:49:03,523 --> 00:49:06,985
Oh, yes, but you don't know
what surprises Bea has in store.

1175
00:49:07,027 --> 00:49:08,570
True.

1176
00:49:08,612 --> 00:49:10,697
True, the board and I
will have a keen eye

1177
00:49:10,739 --> 00:49:11,949
on the audience's reaction
opening night,

1178
00:49:11,990 --> 00:49:14,076
and box office receipts,
of course.

1179
00:49:14,117 --> 00:49:16,036
Of course.
Yes.

1180
00:49:16,078 --> 00:49:19,539
And just wait until you see
how perfect it's all gonna be.

1181
00:49:19,581 --> 00:49:22,292
I myself am dying to see.

1182
00:49:22,334 --> 00:49:23,543
I'm just dying.

1183
00:49:31,385 --> 00:49:33,428
Am I... interrupting?

1184
00:49:33,470 --> 00:49:36,306
Looks like you and the bard are
having an intense discussion.

1185
00:49:36,348 --> 00:49:38,934
He's got a lot to say about
my impostor syndrome.

1186
00:49:38,976 --> 00:49:40,435
"Who do you think you are, Bea?

1187
00:49:40,477 --> 00:49:42,729
"This play is gonna be
a disaster, Bea."

1188
00:49:42,771 --> 00:49:43,939
At least Shakespeare knows

1189
00:49:43,981 --> 00:49:45,357
how to pronounce your name
correctly.

1190
00:49:50,737 --> 00:49:51,947
Where's your witty comeback?

1191
00:49:53,198 --> 00:49:54,783
Mm, trying a new move.

1192
00:49:58,286 --> 00:49:59,579
Looks like you have to sneeze.

1193
00:50:05,669 --> 00:50:07,212
What?

1194
00:50:07,254 --> 00:50:10,173
I never apologized for how
I ended things between us.

1195
00:50:10,215 --> 00:50:11,341
It wasn't right.

1196
00:50:11,383 --> 00:50:13,385
Bea, it's fine.

1197
00:50:13,427 --> 00:50:16,471
It's very obviously not fine
if you're still upset about it.

1198
00:50:16,513 --> 00:50:19,182
I shouldn't have said anything.
I'm sorry.

1199
00:50:19,224 --> 00:50:22,185
Stop apologizing!
I'm the one apologizing here.

1200
00:50:22,227 --> 00:50:23,437
This is my apology time.

1201
00:50:25,355 --> 00:50:26,648
By all means, then.

1202
00:50:30,402 --> 00:50:31,445
I'm sorry.

1203
00:50:32,696 --> 00:50:33,613
Accepted.

1204
00:50:35,073 --> 00:50:36,032
Is that it?

1205
00:50:38,326 --> 00:50:41,455
Why didn't you wanna play
Petruchio with me directing?

1206
00:50:41,496 --> 00:50:43,290
I don't wanna answer
that question right now.

1207
00:50:43,331 --> 00:50:44,416
Why not?

1208
00:50:44,458 --> 00:50:45,959
Because we have bigger problems.

1209
00:50:48,545 --> 00:50:50,422
You think it's possible
that our plan to break up

1210
00:50:50,464 --> 00:50:51,840
Hannah and Clay might have...

1211
00:50:51,882 --> 00:50:53,967
Made things worse
for the play and not better?

1212
00:50:54,009 --> 00:50:55,552
- Do you think?
- Yeah, maybe a little.

1213
00:50:56,762 --> 00:50:58,180
Oh, no.

1214
00:50:58,221 --> 00:51:00,474
I fear had we not meddled,
those two crazy kids

1215
00:51:00,515 --> 00:51:01,641
might have actually
stood a chance.

1216
00:51:01,683 --> 00:51:03,268
We have to fix this.

1217
00:51:03,310 --> 00:51:04,936
The play opens
in less than a week.

1218
00:51:04,978 --> 00:51:06,521
How do we do that?

1219
00:51:06,563 --> 00:51:08,982
We approach them together
and we talk to them.

1220
00:51:09,024 --> 00:51:10,567
As a united front.

1221
00:51:10,609 --> 00:51:13,487
You think the two of us
should advise them on love?

1222
00:51:13,528 --> 00:51:15,197
Maybe we leave it alone.

1223
00:51:15,238 --> 00:51:17,657
Yeah, you know, chances are
it's gonna sort itself out.

1224
00:51:17,699 --> 00:51:18,784
They're both adults.

1225
00:51:18,825 --> 00:51:20,035
Yeah.

1226
00:51:20,077 --> 00:51:21,745
And then worst case,
we'll just trick them

1227
00:51:21,787 --> 00:51:23,079
into falling back in love.

1228
00:51:23,121 --> 00:51:25,332
Oh... that does sound
a whole lot more fun.

1229
00:51:25,373 --> 00:51:27,083
- So, you're in?
- Of course I'm in.

1230
00:51:28,460 --> 00:51:30,629
The masquerade awaits.

1231
00:51:30,670 --> 00:51:32,506
And you, sir, are not invited.

1232
00:51:32,547 --> 00:51:35,634
Ooh! Burn.

1233
00:52:06,790 --> 00:52:08,708
We're gonna have
a lot of work to do

1234
00:52:08,750 --> 00:52:10,794
to get those two back together.

1235
00:52:10,836 --> 00:52:12,963
You clean up nice.
I guess.

1236
00:52:13,004 --> 00:52:13,922
Thanks.

1237
00:52:15,340 --> 00:52:16,800
You look alright, too.

1238
00:52:16,842 --> 00:52:17,759
I guess.

1239
00:52:18,885 --> 00:52:20,053
Thanks.

1240
00:52:20,095 --> 00:52:22,180
- Shall we?
- We shall.

1241
00:52:25,058 --> 00:52:26,935
Party... time.

1242
00:52:41,199 --> 00:52:42,617
This is fun.

1243
00:52:42,659 --> 00:52:44,411
I can't believe
I agreed to this.

1244
00:52:44,452 --> 00:52:46,913
I can't either.
Something's come over you.

1245
00:52:46,955 --> 00:52:48,874
Maybe something
has come over me.

1246
00:52:48,915 --> 00:52:50,375
Like...

1247
00:52:50,417 --> 00:52:51,459
this.

1248
00:52:51,501 --> 00:52:52,836
Yes.

1249
00:52:52,878 --> 00:52:54,546
Game face is on.

1250
00:52:54,588 --> 00:52:55,630
Regroup in ten.

1251
00:52:55,672 --> 00:52:56,756
Aye, aye, Captain.

1252
00:53:10,103 --> 00:53:12,522
Tankard of mead for yourself?

1253
00:53:17,277 --> 00:53:18,445
Thanks.

1254
00:53:18,486 --> 00:53:19,905
You're not having fun, are you?

1255
00:53:19,946 --> 00:53:21,489
No, I am.

1256
00:53:21,531 --> 00:53:23,366
I'm just trying
to stay undistracted.

1257
00:53:24,910 --> 00:53:27,287
The last thing I want
is to disappoint you, Bea.

1258
00:53:27,329 --> 00:53:29,623
Or let the festival down.

1259
00:53:29,664 --> 00:53:32,083
Ah, but you forget,
I believe in you.

1260
00:53:32,125 --> 00:53:33,418
And before I opened my mouth,

1261
00:53:33,460 --> 00:53:35,378
you were doing
a really good job.

1262
00:53:35,420 --> 00:53:36,463
- Really?
- Yeah.

1263
00:53:38,506 --> 00:53:42,052
Well, I think I can do it now.
Benny really helped me out.

1264
00:53:42,093 --> 00:53:44,387
- He did?
- He's amazing.

1265
00:53:46,056 --> 00:53:47,807
He is amazing.

1266
00:53:47,849 --> 00:53:49,726
Ha!
I knew you liked him.

1267
00:53:49,768 --> 00:53:51,353
Drink your mead, lady.

1268
00:53:52,687 --> 00:53:54,439
Oof...

1269
00:53:54,481 --> 00:53:56,566
That was quite the blow-up
at rehearsal today.

1270
00:53:57,817 --> 00:53:59,235
I guess you were right.

1271
00:53:59,277 --> 00:54:01,071
Festival love.

1272
00:54:01,112 --> 00:54:03,406
I... could be wrong.

1273
00:54:03,448 --> 00:54:05,283
And it's the real thing.

1274
00:54:05,325 --> 00:54:06,534
I'm sorry, what?

1275
00:54:06,576 --> 00:54:09,913
I know, buddy,
it seems crazy I can be wrong.

1276
00:54:09,955 --> 00:54:12,290
Alas... there is always
that possibility.

1277
00:54:13,708 --> 00:54:15,585
You know who else is amazing?
Clay.

1278
00:54:18,129 --> 00:54:21,091
I say go for it.

1279
00:54:21,132 --> 00:54:24,928
Let yourself fall in love with
a genuinely good guy who,

1280
00:54:24,970 --> 00:54:28,306
let's face it,
is perfect for you.

1281
00:54:28,348 --> 00:54:30,058
Life is short, and love is rare.

1282
00:54:34,145 --> 00:54:35,188
I don't know.

1283
00:54:39,609 --> 00:54:40,735
It's too late now.

1284
00:54:44,990 --> 00:54:45,991
Ah.

1285
00:54:49,995 --> 00:54:51,371
Go for phase two?

1286
00:54:51,413 --> 00:54:53,164
I really have to dance?

1287
00:54:53,206 --> 00:54:54,916
The time for subtlety
has passed.

1288
00:54:54,958 --> 00:54:57,043
Good luck, soldier.

1289
00:54:57,085 --> 00:54:58,795
May I have this dance?

1290
00:55:00,463 --> 00:55:01,589
Absolutely.

1291
00:55:05,969 --> 00:55:07,429
I don't feel like
dancing right now.

1292
00:55:07,470 --> 00:55:09,222
This is a party,
and you're gonna dance

1293
00:55:09,264 --> 00:55:11,099
whether you like it or not.

1294
00:55:11,141 --> 00:55:13,601
- You sound like my mom.
- Your mom sounds awesome.

1295
00:55:31,870 --> 00:55:33,079
Hi.

1296
00:55:34,539 --> 00:55:35,665
Hi.

1297
00:55:37,500 --> 00:55:41,129
Can you just dance with me
so I can at least apologize?

1298
00:55:41,171 --> 00:55:43,298
I'm the one
who should be apologizing.

1299
00:55:54,434 --> 00:55:55,852
I can't believe that worked.

1300
00:55:55,894 --> 00:55:57,854
I think we should just
let nature take its course

1301
00:55:57,896 --> 00:55:59,314
from now on.

1302
00:55:59,356 --> 00:56:01,399
Listen, as long
as the play is back on track,

1303
00:56:01,441 --> 00:56:03,109
it is fine with me.

1304
00:56:03,151 --> 00:56:05,570
Oh, as if you can put
Bea Bishop, director,

1305
00:56:05,612 --> 00:56:07,405
on pause
for the rest of the night.

1306
00:56:07,447 --> 00:56:10,408
Yes, I'm wearing a mask!
I can be anyone I want.

1307
00:56:10,450 --> 00:56:11,785
Huh.

1308
00:56:11,826 --> 00:56:14,204
Hello.
My name is Sandy.

1309
00:56:14,245 --> 00:56:17,040
I like long walks on the beach
and men with trucks.

1310
00:56:17,082 --> 00:56:18,458
- Hm.
- What's your name?

1311
00:56:18,500 --> 00:56:20,418
I'm Dennis.
I drive a truck.

1312
00:56:37,143 --> 00:56:38,478
My hand is ready.

1313
00:56:38,520 --> 00:56:40,271
May it do him ease.

1314
00:56:40,313 --> 00:56:42,482
Now kiss me, Kate.

1315
00:56:42,524 --> 00:56:45,985
No.
You kiss me, sir.

1316
00:56:46,027 --> 00:56:48,988
Yes, that was it!

1317
00:56:49,030 --> 00:56:51,950
- Bravo!
- Incredible work.

1318
00:56:51,991 --> 00:56:55,036
And I know everybody's very
tired from last night's party,

1319
00:56:55,078 --> 00:56:57,288
so thank you so much
for hanging in there with me.

1320
00:56:57,330 --> 00:57:00,250
Um, Hannah,
you have got these lines down.

1321
00:57:00,291 --> 00:57:02,001
Yeah!

1322
00:57:02,043 --> 00:57:04,796
Now it's just about relaxing
into them, making them your own.

1323
00:57:06,214 --> 00:57:08,758
We share this tomorrow
with an audience.

1324
00:57:11,344 --> 00:57:12,929
Thank you so much, everybody.
Get some rest.

1325
00:57:15,014 --> 00:57:20,103
Hey...

1326
00:57:20,145 --> 00:57:21,271
How was that?

1327
00:57:23,189 --> 00:57:25,733
You can be honest.
Tell me.

1328
00:57:25,775 --> 00:57:27,944
Final dress rehearsal
is always tough.

1329
00:57:27,986 --> 00:57:30,363
You'll have to trust the magic
of this intimate venue

1330
00:57:30,405 --> 00:57:32,198
a little bit.
There's still some time.

1331
00:57:32,240 --> 00:57:34,033
Never enough time.

1332
00:57:34,075 --> 00:57:36,327
You gotta be gentle
with yourself, Bea.

1333
00:57:36,369 --> 00:57:37,912
You know what
you need right now?

1334
00:57:37,954 --> 00:57:39,497
A burger and a beer.

1335
00:57:39,539 --> 00:57:41,207
Ooh, that sounds really good.

1336
00:57:41,249 --> 00:57:42,625
Thanks.

1337
00:57:42,667 --> 00:57:44,002
How about we go for a bite?

1338
00:57:45,378 --> 00:57:47,255
A what?

1339
00:57:47,297 --> 00:57:49,299
Oh, uh, a bite.

1340
00:57:49,340 --> 00:57:50,675
Oh!

1341
00:57:50,717 --> 00:57:53,052
I thought you said "a date."
I was like...

1342
00:57:54,179 --> 00:57:56,347
No, I...
I said "bite."

1343
00:57:56,389 --> 00:57:57,390
Oh...

1344
00:57:57,432 --> 00:58:00,185
But yeah, a date.

1345
00:58:00,226 --> 00:58:01,978
Yes.
Yeah.

1346
00:58:03,813 --> 00:58:05,940
It's working.
Look at them.

1347
00:58:05,982 --> 00:58:07,442
Ah, 20 bucks says
they'll be fighting

1348
00:58:07,483 --> 00:58:08,985
the next time we see them.

1349
00:58:09,027 --> 00:58:10,695
I'd hate to think
what would happen

1350
00:58:10,737 --> 00:58:12,530
if they figured out
we tricked them.

1351
00:58:12,572 --> 00:58:14,449
No, they'll be
so head-over-heels in love

1352
00:58:14,490 --> 00:58:15,950
they won't care, trust me.

1353
00:58:15,992 --> 00:58:17,410
Well, you haven't been
wrong yet, so...

1354
00:58:18,912 --> 00:58:20,205
Is that what you're wearing?

1355
00:58:20,246 --> 00:58:22,165
If you want me to.

1356
00:58:22,207 --> 00:58:24,417
Well, hopefully
Bea can stay focused

1357
00:58:24,459 --> 00:58:26,044
and we'll all be employed
next year.

1358
00:58:26,085 --> 00:58:27,337
Wait,
what are you talking about?

1359
00:58:27,378 --> 00:58:28,630
The buyout offer.

1360
00:58:28,671 --> 00:58:31,216
If Bea isn't approved
to take over from Toni,

1361
00:58:31,257 --> 00:58:32,800
the board's gonna
sell the festival.

1362
00:58:34,510 --> 00:58:36,888
And what's the board
basing Bea's approval on?

1363
00:58:36,930 --> 00:58:38,389
Well, if I were a betting man,

1364
00:58:38,431 --> 00:58:40,767
I'd say on how well we pull off
Taming of the Shrew.

1365
00:58:40,808 --> 00:58:42,268
We have to nail it.

1366
00:58:42,310 --> 00:58:45,855
Yeah, and if we don't succeed,

1367
00:58:45,897 --> 00:58:49,734
this might be our last
Summer Shakespeare Festival.

1368
00:58:49,776 --> 00:58:51,319
No pressure, though, right?

1369
00:58:52,779 --> 00:58:54,155
Yeah.
Yeah, let's do that.

1370
00:58:59,827 --> 00:59:03,206
Huzzah!
My name is Joy Tidings.

1371
00:59:03,248 --> 00:59:04,457
Hi, Joy.

1372
00:59:04,499 --> 00:59:06,709
Here's your wee pint for you,
Benny.

1373
00:59:06,751 --> 00:59:08,336
You know,
when stress levels are high,

1374
00:59:08,378 --> 00:59:09,921
you don't need to do
the whole shtick.

1375
00:59:09,963 --> 00:59:11,547
Oh, thanks, Bea.

1376
00:59:11,589 --> 00:59:14,634
We'll have two Bard Burgers,
medium rare,

1377
00:59:14,676 --> 00:59:17,136
and a Macbeth Scotch Egg.

1378
00:59:17,178 --> 00:59:18,638
One of those for me, please.

1379
00:59:18,680 --> 00:59:20,431
- Thank you.
- Thank you.

1380
00:59:22,016 --> 00:59:24,269
I think this is
our first burger together

1381
00:59:24,310 --> 00:59:26,604
since the night before
Romeo and Juliet opened.

1382
00:59:26,646 --> 00:59:30,692
Oh, you mean the night before
I decided to stop acting.

1383
00:59:32,151 --> 00:59:33,903
The tragedy of Bea Bishop.

1384
00:59:33,945 --> 00:59:35,363
You were a great actor.

1385
00:59:35,405 --> 00:59:36,906
You still are.

1386
00:59:36,948 --> 00:59:38,574
Well, the whole reason
I became an actor

1387
00:59:38,616 --> 00:59:40,285
in the first place was Toni.

1388
00:59:40,326 --> 00:59:42,495
I admire her so much.

1389
00:59:42,537 --> 00:59:45,248
But admiring
doesn't mean copying.

1390
00:59:45,290 --> 00:59:47,250
You know,
that's why I went to New York.

1391
00:59:49,669 --> 00:59:51,671
I always thought you left
because all we had

1392
00:59:51,713 --> 00:59:54,799
was festival love
and nothing more for you.

1393
00:59:54,841 --> 00:59:56,968
That's not what it was for me.

1394
00:59:57,010 --> 00:59:57,885
It broke my heart.

1395
00:59:59,929 --> 01:00:01,681
I wish I could go back in time

1396
01:00:01,723 --> 01:00:03,558
and I could
explain myself better.

1397
01:00:03,599 --> 01:00:06,352
Well, it feels like we're
going back in time right now.

1398
01:00:08,187 --> 01:00:10,356
- Mmm.
- Thanks.

1399
01:00:11,524 --> 01:00:13,943
Well, now we know.

1400
01:00:13,985 --> 01:00:15,445
No going back.

1401
01:00:15,486 --> 01:00:16,446
Only forward.

1402
01:00:16,487 --> 01:00:18,114
Cheers to that.

1403
01:00:18,156 --> 01:00:20,867
And to the new,
brilliant head of the festival.

1404
01:00:26,080 --> 01:00:27,749
All I can hear

1405
01:00:27,790 --> 01:00:29,542
is Hassan
whispering under his breath...

1406
01:00:31,044 --> 01:00:32,295
"I'm so sorry, baby."

1407
01:00:34,130 --> 01:00:37,342
Wait, didn't he marry the
director of that show, Peggy?

1408
01:00:37,383 --> 01:00:38,968
That lady's a legend.

1409
01:00:39,010 --> 01:00:39,927
They're still married!

1410
01:00:39,969 --> 01:00:41,929
No!

1411
01:00:41,971 --> 01:00:43,431
So amazing.

1412
01:00:43,473 --> 01:00:44,807
It's so good to see you laugh.

1413
01:00:44,849 --> 01:00:46,225
Oh.

1414
01:00:46,267 --> 01:00:48,353
Yeah, I almost forgot
tomorrow's opening night

1415
01:00:48,394 --> 01:00:50,063
and my two leads aren't ready,

1416
01:00:50,104 --> 01:00:52,190
and if they don't
knock it out of the park,

1417
01:00:52,231 --> 01:00:54,275
then I might not be
approved as festival head,

1418
01:00:54,317 --> 01:00:56,944
and then, who knows, there
might not even be a festival.

1419
01:00:56,986 --> 01:00:58,071
Oh, pfft.

1420
01:00:58,112 --> 01:00:59,572
You're Bea Bishop.

1421
01:00:59,614 --> 01:01:01,157
You can handle anything
that comes your way.

1422
01:01:01,199 --> 01:01:02,367
Hmm...

1423
01:01:02,408 --> 01:01:05,328
Okay, let me remind you
of the story of you.

1424
01:01:05,370 --> 01:01:09,040
You were unhappy acting, so you
became this amazing director.

1425
01:01:09,082 --> 01:01:11,000
Not only plays, but TV too.

1426
01:01:11,042 --> 01:01:13,127
And now you're gonna come in
and you're gonna revolutionize

1427
01:01:13,169 --> 01:01:14,879
this whole festival.

1428
01:01:14,921 --> 01:01:16,214
It's just pivot after pivot.

1429
01:01:16,255 --> 01:01:17,715
Reinvention.

1430
01:01:17,757 --> 01:01:18,883
Evolution.

1431
01:01:18,925 --> 01:01:21,469
Transformation.

1432
01:01:21,511 --> 01:01:23,721
To me, that much change
is terrifying,

1433
01:01:23,763 --> 01:01:26,224
but you make it look effortless.

1434
01:01:26,265 --> 01:01:27,850
You're an actor, Benny.

1435
01:01:27,892 --> 01:01:29,560
You change all the time
for a living.

1436
01:01:29,602 --> 01:01:31,229
That's different.

1437
01:01:31,270 --> 01:01:35,858
That's being other people
in other situations.

1438
01:01:35,900 --> 01:01:37,318
Sometimes...

1439
01:01:37,360 --> 01:01:39,404
you know, being myself, it's...

1440
01:01:39,445 --> 01:01:42,156
feels like it's...

1441
01:01:42,198 --> 01:01:44,158
impossible.

1442
01:01:44,200 --> 01:01:46,369
Well, I happen to like
who you are.

1443
01:01:46,411 --> 01:01:48,996
And your fellow actors do, too.
They respect you.

1444
01:01:49,038 --> 01:01:50,998
You could take over
the actors' workshop!

1445
01:01:51,040 --> 01:01:53,292
Why are you groaning?

1446
01:01:53,334 --> 01:01:56,254
Why... why is this a bad idea?

1447
01:01:56,295 --> 01:01:57,380
What are you doing?

1448
01:01:57,422 --> 01:01:59,340
- Ah, one second.
- What's happening?

1449
01:02:01,801 --> 01:02:04,095
- Okay.
- What's happening?

1450
01:03:03,863 --> 01:03:05,156
Was winged Cupid blind?

1451
01:04:04,257 --> 01:04:06,050
Let's get outta here.

1452
01:04:06,092 --> 01:04:07,677
- The glasses.
- Oh, why?

1453
01:04:07,718 --> 01:04:09,512
I'm taking this.

1454
01:04:09,554 --> 01:04:10,763
As evidence?

1455
01:04:11,889 --> 01:04:13,391
For posterity.

1456
01:04:16,936 --> 01:04:19,564
They usually wait until Act Two.

1457
01:04:19,605 --> 01:04:21,440
I think they'd wait
until Act Seven or Eight

1458
01:04:21,482 --> 01:04:22,608
if plays had that many acts.

1459
01:04:22,650 --> 01:04:24,110
Right.

1460
01:04:24,151 --> 01:04:26,654
But, in real life,
kisses can happen at any time.

1461
01:04:30,116 --> 01:04:31,242
When you least expect it.

1462
01:04:32,451 --> 01:04:33,995
Like... right now.

1463
01:04:35,871 --> 01:04:37,456
Or, like, right now.

1464
01:04:39,959 --> 01:04:41,878
I'm so happy you told
the girls how you feel.

1465
01:04:44,088 --> 01:04:45,089
Wait, what girls?

1466
01:04:45,131 --> 01:04:46,841
Because I agree.

1467
01:04:46,882 --> 01:04:49,552
We've been drawn to each other

1468
01:04:49,594 --> 01:04:51,637
and we push each other
in a great way.

1469
01:04:51,679 --> 01:04:52,889
I didn't tell
the girls anything.

1470
01:04:54,098 --> 01:04:56,559
Apparently it was a...

1471
01:04:56,601 --> 01:04:59,437
a love... email?

1472
01:04:59,478 --> 01:05:02,773
I'm flattered that you noticed
I've been working out.

1473
01:05:02,815 --> 01:05:04,859
You know, I do feel
stronger, too.

1474
01:05:04,900 --> 01:05:07,320
Wait, someone told you
I have feelings for you?

1475
01:05:07,361 --> 01:05:09,572
I believe the words were...

1476
01:05:10,781 --> 01:05:13,242
"Looks good in a T-shirt".

1477
01:05:13,284 --> 01:05:14,410
But yes.

1478
01:05:14,452 --> 01:05:15,828
Who told you that, Benny?

1479
01:05:15,870 --> 01:05:19,165
Ah, I can't reveal my sources.

1480
01:05:19,206 --> 01:05:20,833
Well, you're the one
who's secretly writing

1481
01:05:20,875 --> 01:05:22,752
love poems, buddy.

1482
01:05:22,793 --> 01:05:24,879
By the way, my hair's
not speckled with anything.

1483
01:05:24,920 --> 01:05:27,882
I'm not... really into poetry.

1484
01:05:27,923 --> 01:05:29,467
And "speckled"?
What?

1485
01:05:29,508 --> 01:05:31,010
That's not the word I would use.

1486
01:05:31,052 --> 01:05:32,386
What word would you use?

1487
01:05:32,428 --> 01:05:34,138
I wouldn't use any word.
I...

1488
01:05:34,180 --> 01:05:37,475
Look, I'm just glad that you
talked to Hannah about me

1489
01:05:37,516 --> 01:05:39,852
because now we've been able to
get everything out in the open.

1490
01:05:39,894 --> 01:05:41,604
You heard I had feelings
for you from Hannah?

1491
01:05:41,646 --> 01:05:42,730
Uh...

1492
01:05:42,772 --> 01:05:45,149
it was more of
an overhear, but yes.

1493
01:05:46,776 --> 01:05:49,737
I overheard Clay and Don say
that you're obsessed with me.

1494
01:05:49,779 --> 01:05:52,281
I'm ob...?
Okay.

1495
01:05:52,323 --> 01:05:53,949
What am I missing here?

1496
01:05:53,991 --> 01:05:56,118
They set us up!

1497
01:05:56,160 --> 01:05:57,787
Wait, they meddled...
with our relationship?

1498
01:05:57,828 --> 01:05:59,246
Why would they do that?

1499
01:05:59,288 --> 01:06:00,665
Maybe because
we meddled with theirs,

1500
01:06:00,706 --> 01:06:02,375
so then they meddled right back?

1501
01:06:02,416 --> 01:06:05,544
Oh, those young, pretty,
meddling tricksters.

1502
01:06:05,586 --> 01:06:07,838
We almost kissed for no reason.

1503
01:06:07,880 --> 01:06:09,590
Wait...

1504
01:06:09,632 --> 01:06:10,800
No reason?

1505
01:06:10,841 --> 01:06:13,761
So, all of this between us,
this was just...

1506
01:06:13,803 --> 01:06:15,137
A trick?

1507
01:06:17,056 --> 01:06:19,225
- One second.
- Don't answer that.

1508
01:06:19,266 --> 01:06:20,851
We should probably
talk this through.

1509
01:06:20,893 --> 01:06:23,229
- Oh, no.
- What is it?

1510
01:06:23,270 --> 01:06:24,397
Hannah quit the play.

1511
01:06:31,904 --> 01:06:32,822
Hannah!

1512
01:06:35,282 --> 01:06:37,034
No, don't try
and change my mind.

1513
01:06:37,076 --> 01:06:38,327
My car is almost here.

1514
01:06:38,369 --> 01:06:39,745
Why are you doing this?

1515
01:06:39,787 --> 01:06:43,290
I didn't know the entire
festival is riding on this play.

1516
01:06:43,332 --> 01:06:45,042
Alright, let's sit down,
let's talk about this.

1517
01:06:45,084 --> 01:06:46,794
Whatever's bothering you,
we can figure it out.

1518
01:06:46,836 --> 01:06:48,629
Don't you hear me?
I'm leaving.

1519
01:06:48,671 --> 01:06:50,131
Okay,
I'm going to an airport hotel

1520
01:06:50,172 --> 01:06:52,049
and I'm booked on a flight out
tomorrow night.

1521
01:06:52,091 --> 01:06:54,635
I am not talented enough
to save this festival.

1522
01:06:54,677 --> 01:06:56,595
It'll be better for you
and the play

1523
01:06:56,637 --> 01:06:58,055
if you let Maggie take over.

1524
01:06:58,097 --> 01:07:00,474
Everybody's coming to see you.

1525
01:07:00,516 --> 01:07:03,769
The entire run of the show
is sold out.

1526
01:07:03,811 --> 01:07:05,855
No, it's...
it's too much pressure.

1527
01:07:05,896 --> 01:07:08,315
I promise
I will make it up to you, Bea.

1528
01:07:08,357 --> 01:07:09,650
How?

1529
01:07:09,692 --> 01:07:11,819
I got offered a role
in a limited series.

1530
01:07:11,861 --> 01:07:13,779
It's about a luxury resort

1531
01:07:13,821 --> 01:07:16,741
during World War II
in Portofino.

1532
01:07:16,782 --> 01:07:18,784
Whoa, that sounds really good.

1533
01:07:18,826 --> 01:07:20,494
It is.

1534
01:07:20,536 --> 01:07:22,913
I told my agents to
put you up for directing on it.

1535
01:07:22,955 --> 01:07:25,958
But I'm supposed to be
taking over the festival.

1536
01:07:26,000 --> 01:07:27,668
But is that
what you really want?

1537
01:07:27,710 --> 01:07:29,712
Because if not...

1538
01:07:29,754 --> 01:07:32,506
get on a plane,
come to the Italian coast.

1539
01:07:32,548 --> 01:07:34,425
Hey, I've been where you are,

1540
01:07:34,467 --> 01:07:36,719
and feeling like the only thing
you can do is run away,

1541
01:07:36,761 --> 01:07:37,970
but listen to me.

1542
01:07:38,012 --> 01:07:39,555
Whatever you're
running away from,

1543
01:07:39,597 --> 01:07:40,765
it's gonna catch up to you.

1544
01:07:43,851 --> 01:07:45,561
- I have to go. I'm sorry.
- Hannah...

1545
01:07:50,441 --> 01:07:51,692
Thank you.

1546
01:07:51,734 --> 01:07:52,735
Hannah...

1547
01:08:05,122 --> 01:08:06,874
I'm sorry it's so late,
everyone.

1548
01:08:06,916 --> 01:08:08,417
Okay,
what is with all the SOS texts?

1549
01:08:08,459 --> 01:08:09,752
Hannah quit.

1550
01:08:10,753 --> 01:08:12,004
She left.

1551
01:08:13,255 --> 01:08:14,715
Okay, it's gonna be fine.

1552
01:08:14,757 --> 01:08:16,884
I'll call and text her,
try to change her mind, Bea.

1553
01:08:16,926 --> 01:08:18,511
On the off chance that
that doesn't work,

1554
01:08:18,552 --> 01:08:20,387
we need to come up
with a backup plan.

1555
01:08:20,429 --> 01:08:22,181
Tomorrow night, we'll tell
everyone that Hannah is ill

1556
01:08:22,223 --> 01:08:24,183
and the part of Kate
will be played by Maggie.

1557
01:08:24,225 --> 01:08:26,268
Oh, no.
No, no, no, no, I can't.

1558
01:08:26,310 --> 01:08:27,937
You're the understudy.

1559
01:08:27,978 --> 01:08:29,939
Understudies are never ready
this early on in any play's run.

1560
01:08:29,980 --> 01:08:31,023
You know that.

1561
01:08:31,065 --> 01:08:32,316
Could we postpone the opening?

1562
01:08:32,358 --> 01:08:34,276
The entire future
of the festival's at risk.

1563
01:08:34,318 --> 01:08:37,196
There's only one logical option
at this point, Bea.

1564
01:08:37,238 --> 01:08:39,114
- What?
- You're playing Kate.

1565
01:08:41,867 --> 01:08:42,785
I'm what?

1566
01:08:45,079 --> 01:08:48,165
Vail your stomachs,
for it is no boot

1567
01:08:48,207 --> 01:08:51,335
to place your hand
below your husband's foot,

1568
01:08:51,377 --> 01:08:53,754
in token in...
of which duty...

1569
01:08:53,796 --> 01:08:55,214
No, we changed that.

1570
01:08:55,256 --> 01:08:57,883
What?
I don't... I...

1571
01:08:57,925 --> 01:08:59,844
Why did I think I could do this?

1572
01:08:59,885 --> 01:09:02,054
- Because you can.
- History is repeating itself.

1573
01:09:02,096 --> 01:09:05,015
No, it's not.
This is gonna work.

1574
01:09:05,057 --> 01:09:07,476
You have the lines, but you're
thinking like Bea the director.

1575
01:09:07,518 --> 01:09:10,104
If you're gonna be Kate,
you have to think like Kate.

1576
01:09:10,145 --> 01:09:12,815
Be here.
Alright, with your Petruchio.

1577
01:09:14,483 --> 01:09:15,401
Partner's foot.

1578
01:09:19,697 --> 01:09:23,784
And place your hands
below your partner's foot.

1579
01:09:23,826 --> 01:09:27,663
In token of which duty,
if she please.

1580
01:09:28,873 --> 01:09:32,084
My hand is ready,
may it do her ease.

1581
01:09:33,919 --> 01:09:35,880
Come on.
Kiss me, Kate.

1582
01:09:37,506 --> 01:09:39,383
You would have made
a brilliant Petruchio.

1583
01:09:43,888 --> 01:09:45,306
Please tell me this is working.

1584
01:09:46,390 --> 01:09:48,475
It was, Doug.

1585
01:09:48,517 --> 01:09:50,102
Yeah,
let's take it from the top.

1586
01:09:50,144 --> 01:09:51,228
- Tick-tock.
- Yeah, it's good.

1587
01:09:51,270 --> 01:09:52,354
Yeah, thank you, Doug.

1588
01:09:52,396 --> 01:09:54,690
Thank you.
Good job.

1589
01:09:54,732 --> 01:09:56,442
- Okay.
- Good.

1590
01:09:56,483 --> 01:09:59,403
To make a stale of me among...
No!

1591
01:09:59,445 --> 01:10:02,198
To make a stale of me
amongst these mates.

1592
01:10:02,239 --> 01:10:04,825
Ugh, I cannot believe
I'm still learning these lines

1593
01:10:04,867 --> 01:10:06,076
the day of the show!

1594
01:10:06,118 --> 01:10:07,453
I've been texting her all day.

1595
01:10:07,494 --> 01:10:10,372
I even left a voice memo
of me reciting a sonnet.

1596
01:10:10,414 --> 01:10:12,041
She ghosted me.

1597
01:10:12,082 --> 01:10:14,043
You know this has
nothing to do with you, right?

1598
01:10:14,084 --> 01:10:15,377
This is about Hannah
and her confidence.

1599
01:10:15,419 --> 01:10:17,087
She's afraid.

1600
01:10:17,129 --> 01:10:18,714
Yeah, I guess just keep trying.

1601
01:10:20,341 --> 01:10:21,508
Wow.

1602
01:10:23,344 --> 01:10:26,555
By the way, I haven't had
a chance to say this, but...

1603
01:10:26,597 --> 01:10:29,099
I think you'd make
a great festival head.

1604
01:10:29,141 --> 01:10:30,392
Regardless of what Benny says.

1605
01:10:31,852 --> 01:10:33,020
What did Benny say?

1606
01:10:33,062 --> 01:10:35,689
Oh, that you had
impossible shoes to fill.

1607
01:10:35,731 --> 01:10:38,734
How's everybody feeling, huh?

1608
01:10:38,776 --> 01:10:42,238
Will you go and run
Act Four with Clay

1609
01:10:42,279 --> 01:10:44,156
while I figure out
what to do with my hair?

1610
01:10:44,198 --> 01:10:46,242
- Of course.
- This is a nightmare.

1611
01:10:46,283 --> 01:10:47,618
Yeah, I'll be right there.

1612
01:10:48,953 --> 01:10:50,204
You got this.

1613
01:10:50,246 --> 01:10:52,081
No, I definitely
do not got this.

1614
01:10:52,122 --> 01:10:54,208
You look amazing,
so you're already halfway there.

1615
01:10:54,250 --> 01:10:55,668
No, that's not
what this is about.

1616
01:10:55,709 --> 01:10:57,461
I don't belong on that stage.

1617
01:10:57,503 --> 01:11:00,089
I belong backstage
in jeans and a baseball cap.

1618
01:11:00,130 --> 01:11:01,382
I learned that years ago.

1619
01:11:01,423 --> 01:11:03,259
That's what Romeo and Juliet
taught me.

1620
01:11:03,300 --> 01:11:05,594
You're gonna be
head of the festival next.

1621
01:11:05,636 --> 01:11:06,553
Am I?

1622
01:11:08,305 --> 01:11:09,974
Or do I have
"impossible shoes to fill"?

1623
01:11:11,266 --> 01:11:12,226
What?

1624
01:11:13,394 --> 01:11:14,687
You said that to Clay.

1625
01:11:14,728 --> 01:11:16,522
That was just
a figure of speech.

1626
01:11:16,563 --> 01:11:17,481
Hm?

1627
01:11:19,233 --> 01:11:21,485
Toni's set a high standard.

1628
01:11:25,155 --> 01:11:27,241
So, that is how you really feel.

1629
01:11:31,036 --> 01:11:33,539
You know, you believing in me
made me believe in myself,

1630
01:11:33,580 --> 01:11:35,582
which made me feel like
I deserved this job

1631
01:11:35,624 --> 01:11:37,459
because I'd be actually
good at it.

1632
01:11:38,961 --> 01:11:41,839
And now I feel like I should
just follow Hannah to Italy.

1633
01:11:41,880 --> 01:11:43,382
- Italy?
- Yeah.

1634
01:11:44,633 --> 01:11:46,385
'Cause at least there
I'd be doing a job

1635
01:11:46,427 --> 01:11:48,679
I know I'm actually good at.

1636
01:11:48,721 --> 01:11:50,097
Where would that leave us?

1637
01:11:55,352 --> 01:11:57,896
That leaves us as two people
who fell for a trick, Benny.

1638
01:12:01,817 --> 01:12:04,361
I guess it does.

1639
01:12:04,403 --> 01:12:05,529
Silly us.

1640
01:12:08,073 --> 01:12:09,116
<i>Okay, everybody,</i>

1641
01:12:09,158 --> 01:12:10,284
<i>this is your three-hour warning.</i>

1642
01:12:10,326 --> 01:12:11,827
<i>We only have three hours
till showtime.</i>

1643
01:12:18,834 --> 01:12:22,254
Say that she rail;
why, then I'll tell her plain...

1644
01:12:23,505 --> 01:12:26,425
she sings as sweetly
as a nightingale.

1645
01:12:29,720 --> 01:12:31,513
Okay.

1646
01:12:31,555 --> 01:12:32,514
Let's do this.

1647
01:12:33,766 --> 01:12:35,392
You good?

1648
01:12:35,434 --> 01:12:37,061
Yeah.

1649
01:12:37,102 --> 01:12:38,020
I think...

1650
01:12:39,646 --> 01:12:41,523
Look, I just wanted to say,

1651
01:12:41,565 --> 01:12:42,983
you really helped me
with this part, Benny.

1652
01:12:44,735 --> 01:12:45,736
You're welcome.

1653
01:12:45,778 --> 01:12:47,571
It's really cool
to have a mentor.

1654
01:12:47,613 --> 01:12:49,364
It's cool to be one.

1655
01:12:49,406 --> 01:12:52,368
I still can't believe we're
doing this without Hannah.

1656
01:12:52,409 --> 01:12:54,953
If the lines get messy,

1657
01:12:54,995 --> 01:12:56,872
just play it off like
Kate and Petruchio

1658
01:12:56,914 --> 01:12:58,957
are on the same
secret wavelength at all times.

1659
01:12:58,999 --> 01:13:00,793
Like you and Bea?

1660
01:13:03,170 --> 01:13:04,463
Once this is all over,

1661
01:13:04,505 --> 01:13:07,299
remind me how mad I am
at all of you,

1662
01:13:07,341 --> 01:13:09,968
making Bea and me believe that
we were in love with each other.

1663
01:13:10,010 --> 01:13:11,804
Was it really a trick
if it's true?

1664
01:13:15,015 --> 01:13:16,600
How many hours until places?

1665
01:13:16,642 --> 01:13:18,227
Uh, less than two at this point.

1666
01:13:30,906 --> 01:13:32,032
Hi, how are you?

1667
01:13:50,342 --> 01:13:52,553
Juan and the whole board
are here.

1668
01:13:52,594 --> 01:13:54,513
The entire run of the show
is officially sold out

1669
01:13:54,555 --> 01:13:56,348
and it's only opening night!

1670
01:13:56,390 --> 01:13:58,434
Okay, everybody,
this is your places call

1671
01:13:58,475 --> 01:13:59,852
for the top of the show.

1672
01:13:59,893 --> 01:14:01,854
Wait, has anybody
laid eyes on Benny?

1673
01:14:01,895 --> 01:14:04,022
Uh, I haven't actually
seen him in a while.

1674
01:14:04,064 --> 01:14:05,440
What?!

1675
01:14:05,482 --> 01:14:07,443
First Hannah, now Benny?
This can't be happening!

1676
01:14:08,610 --> 01:14:10,279
I'm here!

1677
01:14:10,320 --> 01:14:11,989
- Hannah!
- Bea, I'm here.

1678
01:14:12,030 --> 01:14:13,490
What changed your mind?

1679
01:14:13,532 --> 01:14:15,659
I was going through airport
security and all of a sudden

1680
01:14:15,701 --> 01:14:17,661
I hear this voice yell,
"Don't get on the plane.

1681
01:14:17,703 --> 01:14:19,788
"We need you."
It was like I was in a rom-com.

1682
01:14:19,830 --> 01:14:21,790
It wasn't me.

1683
01:14:21,832 --> 01:14:22,875
It was Benny.

1684
01:14:24,710 --> 01:14:25,919
Benny?

1685
01:14:25,961 --> 01:14:27,754
Did you run through the airport
in your costume?

1686
01:14:27,796 --> 01:14:28,714
Yep.

1687
01:14:30,174 --> 01:14:31,800
Hannah, can you go on?

1688
01:14:31,842 --> 01:14:32,885
Of course.

1689
01:14:32,926 --> 01:14:34,428
Alright,
let's get you in this dress!

1690
01:14:34,470 --> 01:14:35,888
Doug Berrie,
I need you to stall.

1691
01:14:35,929 --> 01:14:38,348
The play has started.

1692
01:14:38,390 --> 01:14:39,892
Yes, except that Kate
doesn't go on until

1693
01:14:39,933 --> 01:14:41,852
after Lucentio's monologue.

1694
01:14:41,894 --> 01:14:45,272
And Lucentio is in a very
slow-talking mode right, Don?

1695
01:14:45,314 --> 01:14:46,481
Got it, Bea.

1696
01:14:46,523 --> 01:14:48,525
Alright, come on, let's go.
Come on!

1697
01:14:49,902 --> 01:14:51,111
Okay...

1698
01:14:52,196 --> 01:14:55,532
Okay...
Alright!

1699
01:14:58,368 --> 01:15:00,412
And practice rhetoric
in your common talk...

1700
01:15:01,955 --> 01:15:03,415
I've thought about
what you said.

1701
01:15:03,457 --> 01:15:05,000
About not running away.

1702
01:15:05,042 --> 01:15:07,878
Everyone here has worked
so hard, and I realized...

1703
01:15:07,920 --> 01:15:09,546
I just want to make you proud.

1704
01:15:09,588 --> 01:15:12,049
I couldn't possibly be
more proud of you.

1705
01:15:12,090 --> 01:15:13,926
And I'm so sorry
if I made you feel

1706
01:15:13,967 --> 01:15:16,011
distracted or unfocused or...

1707
01:15:16,053 --> 01:15:17,179
Well, I was distracted.

1708
01:15:17,221 --> 01:15:18,639
And unfocused.

1709
01:15:18,680 --> 01:15:21,141
But I think that's where
I'm at my best.

1710
01:15:21,183 --> 01:15:23,227
And I think that's where
Kate is at her best, too.

1711
01:15:23,268 --> 01:15:24,144
What do you mean?

1712
01:15:25,312 --> 01:15:27,356
Dealing with her
parents' legacy...

1713
01:15:27,397 --> 01:15:30,150
a life that's short
where love is rare.

1714
01:15:30,192 --> 01:15:31,735
It's just one big juggling act.

1715
01:15:31,777 --> 01:15:34,404
No one's ever gonna be
perfect at it, right?

1716
01:15:34,446 --> 01:15:36,198
Go kill it out there.

1717
01:15:36,240 --> 01:15:38,242
I always do.

1718
01:15:39,451 --> 01:15:42,079
Thou must be married
to no man but me.

1719
01:15:43,538 --> 01:15:46,416
For I am he
am born to tame you, Kate.

1720
01:15:48,168 --> 01:15:51,338
And I am she
am born to tame you, sir.

1721
01:15:51,380 --> 01:15:53,840
And I'll go from
a wild Kate to a Kate

1722
01:15:53,882 --> 01:15:55,592
conformable as other
household Kates...

1723
01:15:57,010 --> 01:15:59,388
Hannah's doing beautifully.

1724
01:15:59,429 --> 01:16:01,974
The audience is eating
out of the palm of her hand.

1725
01:16:02,015 --> 01:16:04,351
I knew she had it in her.

1726
01:16:04,393 --> 01:16:06,270
I can't believe
you brought her back.

1727
01:16:06,311 --> 01:16:08,146
Why'd you do that?

1728
01:16:08,188 --> 01:16:09,106
Why do you think?

1729
01:16:10,649 --> 01:16:12,109
I'd do anything to help you,
Bea.

1730
01:16:14,611 --> 01:16:16,822
You belong here.

1731
01:16:16,863 --> 01:16:19,658
And you deserve to be here.

1732
01:16:19,700 --> 01:16:22,286
As the head of the festival.

1733
01:16:22,327 --> 01:16:24,621
This is what you want, isn't it?

1734
01:16:24,663 --> 01:16:26,582
Unless I'm wrong.

1735
01:16:26,623 --> 01:16:29,543
Maybe what you really want
is in Italy.

1736
01:16:29,585 --> 01:16:31,003
You missed your cue!

1737
01:16:31,044 --> 01:16:33,046
See how they kiss and court!

1738
01:16:34,339 --> 01:16:37,175
Signor Lucentio,
here is my hand.

1739
01:16:37,217 --> 01:16:38,176
Hey.

1740
01:16:39,845 --> 01:16:42,306
There you are.
What are you doing out here?

1741
01:16:42,347 --> 01:16:43,432
Oh...

1742
01:16:43,473 --> 01:16:45,142
Don't you wanna see the ending?

1743
01:16:45,183 --> 01:16:46,393
Has the ending changed?

1744
01:16:47,811 --> 01:16:50,147
Well, you never know.
People can surprise you.

1745
01:16:50,188 --> 01:16:51,106
Oh, that's true.

1746
01:16:55,485 --> 01:17:00,907
Mom, do you remember the review
that I got for Romeo and Juliet?

1747
01:17:00,949 --> 01:17:04,953
The one that called you
"distracted and uninspired"?

1748
01:17:04,995 --> 01:17:06,496
That's the one.

1749
01:17:06,538 --> 01:17:09,541
Mom, that review
is the reason...

1750
01:17:09,583 --> 01:17:11,376
that I decided to stop acting.

1751
01:17:11,418 --> 01:17:14,254
Because how could I possibly

1752
01:17:14,296 --> 01:17:16,631
live up to the great
Toni Bishop?

1753
01:17:16,673 --> 01:17:17,924
Oh...

1754
01:17:17,966 --> 01:17:20,677
Mm-hm...
Honey...

1755
01:17:20,719 --> 01:17:24,097
You know, your success here

1756
01:17:24,139 --> 01:17:26,892
is not about what you're doing.

1757
01:17:26,933 --> 01:17:29,645
It's all about who you are.

1758
01:17:29,686 --> 01:17:33,148
And you are the leader
of this festival.

1759
01:17:35,067 --> 01:17:35,942
Thank you, Mom.

1760
01:17:41,073 --> 01:17:43,367
You wanna tell me
what's really going on?

1761
01:17:47,204 --> 01:17:48,121
Benny.

1762
01:17:49,664 --> 01:17:50,666
Ah-hah.

1763
01:17:53,502 --> 01:17:54,628
Bea...

1764
01:17:56,129 --> 01:17:58,632
You were always
my first priority.

1765
01:18:00,342 --> 01:18:03,011
But after you...

1766
01:18:03,053 --> 01:18:05,180
I was married to my job.

1767
01:18:05,222 --> 01:18:06,681
Mm.

1768
01:18:06,723 --> 01:18:09,559
This festival was my great love.

1769
01:18:09,601 --> 01:18:12,020
But...

1770
01:18:12,062 --> 01:18:13,355
if I have any regret,

1771
01:18:13,397 --> 01:18:16,441
it's that I didn't
take care of my heart.

1772
01:18:16,483 --> 01:18:17,401
Hm.

1773
01:18:20,112 --> 01:18:21,655
Take care of your heart, honey.

1774
01:18:26,535 --> 01:18:28,662
He drives me nuts.

1775
01:18:30,956 --> 01:18:33,375
You drive him nuts.

1776
01:18:35,794 --> 01:18:37,212
It's nothing but love.

1777
01:18:38,713 --> 01:18:42,717
Sometimes you have to realize
that the only thing

1778
01:18:42,759 --> 01:18:45,095
standing in your way...

1779
01:18:45,137 --> 01:18:46,263
is you.

1780
01:18:53,478 --> 01:18:55,897
Oh, the play's ending soon.

1781
01:18:57,733 --> 01:19:01,278
Hey, Mom, why don't you
come out on that stage with me?

1782
01:19:01,319 --> 01:19:04,072
Let's give them
one more bow here tonight.

1783
01:19:05,991 --> 01:19:08,076
No, this is your moment, baby.

1784
01:19:08,118 --> 01:19:09,619
You go enjoy it.

1785
01:19:15,375 --> 01:19:17,461
For it is no boot,

1786
01:19:17,502 --> 01:19:19,838
to place your hand
below your partner's foot,

1787
01:19:21,089 --> 01:19:23,842
in token of which duty,
if she please.

1788
01:19:25,761 --> 01:19:28,054
My hand is ready,
may it do him ease.

1789
01:19:29,389 --> 01:19:32,100
Come on and kiss me, Kate.

1790
01:19:32,142 --> 01:19:34,060
No, you kiss me, sir.

1791
01:19:58,919 --> 01:20:02,380
Thank you!

1792
01:20:17,437 --> 01:20:19,940
Hi!
Congratulations.

1793
01:20:19,981 --> 01:20:21,233
Great job.

1794
01:20:24,402 --> 01:20:25,946
Mm, thank you!

1795
01:20:25,987 --> 01:20:27,280
You're welcome.

1796
01:20:29,616 --> 01:20:30,951
You were great, by the way.

1797
01:20:34,746 --> 01:20:36,081
Hi.

1798
01:20:36,122 --> 01:20:39,000
An absolutely wonderful
opening night, Bea.

1799
01:20:39,042 --> 01:20:41,253
You're right, I admit I was
skeptical about Hannah Michaels,

1800
01:20:41,294 --> 01:20:42,546
but she really delivered.

1801
01:20:42,587 --> 01:20:44,923
Everyone did.
But especially you.

1802
01:20:44,965 --> 01:20:46,550
Thank you so much.

1803
01:20:46,591 --> 01:20:48,552
The subtle changes were clever.

1804
01:20:48,593 --> 01:20:50,679
You made this look easy,
which is no surprise.

1805
01:20:50,720 --> 01:20:52,597
Yeah, no, not a surprise to me.

1806
01:20:52,639 --> 01:20:54,349
The entire audience
was enthralled.

1807
01:20:54,391 --> 01:20:56,059
And the board?

1808
01:20:56,101 --> 01:20:58,436
We all agree the future
of the festival is safe

1809
01:20:58,478 --> 01:21:00,188
in your capable hands, Bea.

1810
01:21:00,230 --> 01:21:01,856
Thank you, Juan.

1811
01:21:01,898 --> 01:21:03,567
I couldn't agree more.

1812
01:21:03,608 --> 01:21:05,652
I won't let you down, sir.

1813
01:21:05,694 --> 01:21:07,070
Thank you very much.

1814
01:21:07,112 --> 01:21:07,988
Excuse me.

1815
01:21:13,243 --> 01:21:14,744
Hi!

1816
01:21:14,786 --> 01:21:15,954
I've been looking for you.

1817
01:21:17,872 --> 01:21:19,291
You were?

1818
01:21:19,332 --> 01:21:21,167
What for?

1819
01:21:21,209 --> 01:21:23,670
What did you say to Hannah
to get her to come back?

1820
01:21:23,712 --> 01:21:25,255
Oh, you mean after
I caught my breath?

1821
01:21:25,297 --> 01:21:26,715
Yeah.

1822
01:21:26,756 --> 01:21:30,093
I told her how we were both
so lucky to know you.

1823
01:21:30,135 --> 01:21:32,721
And that she didn't
need to worry about failing.

1824
01:21:32,762 --> 01:21:35,807
All she had to do was remember
that Bea Bishop believes in her.

1825
01:21:37,642 --> 01:21:39,436
She doesn't need anything else.

1826
01:21:40,854 --> 01:21:44,858
You make people wanna be brave.

1827
01:21:44,899 --> 01:21:46,693
And if I was,

1828
01:21:46,735 --> 01:21:50,280
I'd admit that I love mentoring.

1829
01:21:50,322 --> 01:21:53,742
But that doesn't mean
that I can't act as well.

1830
01:21:53,783 --> 01:21:55,619
If you believe in me...

1831
01:21:55,660 --> 01:21:56,912
I think I can do anything.

1832
01:21:59,205 --> 01:22:00,206
I have another question.

1833
01:22:01,458 --> 01:22:03,418
Of course you do.

1834
01:22:03,460 --> 01:22:05,545
Why didn't you wanna play
Petruchio with me directing?

1835
01:22:05,587 --> 01:22:07,172
Because it's true.

1836
01:22:07,213 --> 01:22:09,341
I've never gotten over you, Bea.

1837
01:22:09,382 --> 01:22:11,134
All these years...

1838
01:22:11,176 --> 01:22:13,178
I still loved you.

1839
01:22:13,219 --> 01:22:15,597
And I thought
working that closely,

1840
01:22:15,639 --> 01:22:19,184
it would be impossible
to hide my feelings for you.

1841
01:22:19,225 --> 01:22:21,603
As it turns out...

1842
01:22:21,645 --> 01:22:22,771
I was right.

1843
01:22:23,897 --> 01:22:25,440
I'm glad you were right.

1844
01:22:27,025 --> 01:22:29,319
- Any more questions?
- No.

1845
01:22:29,361 --> 01:22:30,236
But I have answers.

1846
01:22:32,489 --> 01:22:33,782
I'm not gonna go to Italy.

1847
01:22:35,492 --> 01:22:37,744
I'm gonna stay right here...

1848
01:22:37,786 --> 01:22:39,454
and I'm gonna run this festival.

1849
01:22:40,789 --> 01:22:42,916
New moves.

1850
01:22:42,957 --> 01:22:44,209
Smart.

1851
01:22:44,250 --> 01:22:46,795
And I want to be with you.

1852
01:22:46,836 --> 01:22:48,713
I'm with you, honey.

1853
01:22:48,755 --> 01:22:51,341
Even when I give you
a hard time?

1854
01:22:51,383 --> 01:22:53,259
Especially then.

1855
01:22:53,301 --> 01:22:55,512
New moves, indeed.

1856
01:22:55,553 --> 01:22:59,265
But you know I'm gonna
need help running this place.

1857
01:22:59,307 --> 01:23:02,268
And I think you and I
are a lot stronger together.

1858
01:23:02,310 --> 01:23:03,770
Well...

1859
01:23:03,812 --> 01:23:05,689
I could consider it.

1860
01:23:05,730 --> 01:23:08,316
I mean, as long as you
run things strictly according

1861
01:23:08,358 --> 01:23:09,401
to classical tradition.

1862
01:23:09,442 --> 01:23:11,528
Oh, kiss me, you fool.